The first virtual operator will already arrive in the Czech Republic this year.
Probably
The mobile market in the Czech Republic is well developed and our operators offer virtually all the services like their Western European partners.
However, here we still lack something - the virtual operator, that would force those to be cheaper.
Probably won't be here.
That Czech operators are among the most expensive in Europe, we've already written on mobil.
It was also confirmed by the Czech Telecommunications Office, which warned that the price of SMS messages in our country is one of the most expensive in the whole Union.
Because of the European Commission's last steps in the field of the regulation of roaming charges a paradoxical situation is likely to come to a paradoxical situation for some Czech mobile operators the SMS they ship from abroad will be cheaper than if they send them from home.
How is it possible that prices in the Czech Republic will remain so high compared to Europe?
The answer is simple, there's no competition I really want to fight.
Probably seems paradoxical, because we have three mobile networks and their high coverage is more than 100 per cent.
On the contrary, mobile operators like to lament that there is Extreme Competence in the Czech market.
This is also the Chairman of the Council of the Czech Telecommunications Office, Pavel Dvoák, who has already stated that there is already no place in the Czech Republic for any other operator.
It is perhaps true that for a classic operator with its own network it no longer has too many chances (for the rest, it would be very difficult to find some frequency).
However, there is still a possibility - the virtual operator.
Precise Precise the entry of the virtual operator into the mobile telephony market resulted in a significant decrease in prices to which classic operators should also react.
At the same time, even before sharks such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile entered the market in Great Britain, similar voices were heard both there and here.
It has been shown that there is a place for this type of operator and that they can be successful.
Moreover, there is no need to look for examples too far.
In February of this this year we informed you about the premiere of virtual operator Mobilking in Poland.
Only after half a year, this operator managed to obtain more than 100 thousand customers and become the fifth largest operator in the neighboring country.
On the other hand, after Mobilking there is the Penta group that, apart from it, in the Czech Republic is the owner of U:fon.
The advantage of the virtual operators lies in the possibility of concentrating on many more specific target groups.
In addition, in order for them to function effectively, they are enough with far fewer customers than a classic operator.
They don't carry relatively large costs in the construction, operation and maintenance of the network.
In the world these types of virtual operators are usually profiled with low expenses - they offer cheap calls and SMS messaging that, however, is often offset by the offer of tighter services.
For example, Tesco mobile in Britain offers SMS at least as much as half as cheap relative to the competition.
However, as the Mobilking example in Poland shows it may also be a good blow the berth for an untrivial target group.
This operator specifically targets the man.
"The right man chooses Mobilking - it's the operator he likes and who can be trusted with," says David Seibert, the CEO of Mobile Entertainment Company, which works this virtual operator.
It's hard to say whether there's a similar concept like that of Poland could hook in our country.
Vodafone spokesman Miroslav Eepick is in doubt: "The Czech market is relatively small to access by segments on which most virtual operators are based.
In addition from the point of view of the supply of services it is relatively saturated."
In the Czech Republic there is no possibility, mobile operators say
However, it seems almost certain that if in the Czech Republic there will be a virtual operator that it will not be Mobilking.
"We are talking about the expansion of this successful project, however, in the Czech Republic it doesn't seem too hopeful.
Rather, it's Mobilking launch in other countries of our region," said Jana Studnie, spokeswoman for the Penta financial group, in a telephone conversation for mobil.
Indirectly he assured that the current mobile phone operators in the Czech Republic definitely do not bow to this idea.
On the contrary, they are trying to do everything they can to ensure that no potential competition emerges.
Interest about the launch of a virtual mobile operator is already demonstrated by the GTS Novera group that it would like to take a step further to become a real competition of Telefónica O2 in the field of company customers.
"We have a lot of interest in it, to be able to offer our customers not only fixed telephony services but also mobile telephony services.
As soon as possible, the better.
With this possibility we have repeatedly met with all Czech mobile phone operators.
With some of them we have managed to make progress in the meetings," group spokesman Pavel Kaidl said.
Then they might flash better times.
It seems that, due to unofficial information, the first official operator would possibly have to arrive at the end of the year by the end of the year.
But for now his apparition is accompanied by an impenetrable wall of silence.
"At the moment we believe that the situation is suitable for the emergence of a virtual operator in the Czech market.
The Czech market compared to others is a very specific market.
So far no one has approached us with a complex and serious project in this area," Vlastimil Sr., spokesman for Telefónica O2.
The specific nature of the Czech market refers to other operators.
T-mobile spokeswoman Martina Kemrová, in exceptional compliance with O2's opinion, adds: "Until today we haven't registered any viable commercial projects that, bilaterally profitable, can continue to develop.
He adds: "On the T-Mobile network it will be difficult to find a virtual operator until the end of the year.
At the moment we should already have on the table some concrete contract that could be studied on such a short horizon."
Vodafone turns its attention instead to complying with the requirements proper to a 3G license.
"We are open to negotiations with potential virtual operators and this with regard to the distribution of expenses and infrastructure.
Precisely we regard it as the greatest contribution from the point of view of customers.
Vodafone's main strategy in this field is the partner with which to establish a shared network for a fast internet on the mobile."
Responsible offices - with nail-and-meat operators
The progress of mobile operators can be easily understood, but the input of another operator would mean increasing competition which would lead to a drop in prices and thus a decrease in profits.
Then why make it easier to enter the competition?
"When the operators want to achieve something, they are able to pull the same string, proof of this is the grip when U:fon."
"Perhaps the greatest pressure to the "T" was at the time of the entry of U:fon for the T-Mobile society.
Attacks this is attested by the public information accessible on the web pages of the .
But right now U: Phone serves as an argument on why not accept a virtual operator.
Martina Kemrová adds to this: "In addition, the competitive atmosphere in the mobile telephony market has not long been opened to a fourth operator.
With regard to the nature of the mobile telephony market in the Czech Republic it is natural that the entry of another player into the market would be for him and, above all, for his services great requirements."
That is why this access to mobile operators is tremendously discouraging due to the absolute disgrace of the Czech regulatory body.
It's not just the position of ETU, which unfortunately has no competence to intervene in commercial tenders, unlike Great Britain and other developed countries.
This purely belongs to the Office for the Protection of Commercial Licitations (OHS).
However, it expressed its typical position on this problem.
"The entry of a virtual operator into the mobile telephony market would be possible after the conclusion of an agreement on the rental of the necessary infrastructure between a mobile phone operator and a virtual operator.
Until today no agreement has been closed since the offer of the potential stakeholders rather was not interesting enough for the classic operators," Kristián Chalupa of the Department of External Relations of OHS added to our question.
These statements do not surprise the representatives of the operators.
For the offices that must represent the interests of consumers it is something more than a consideration, apart from that they must note that no one takes advantage of their position on the market.
Especially when Kristián Chalupa adds: "Oversely abroad where virtual operators operate normally we can see that their entry into the market contributed to the lower prices of mobile phone services."
Evidently, the pressure in terms of the proper functioning of the means of the market and the fall in prices do not fall within the interest of OHS.
Actually what's dramatically shocking in this whole case is the dissonant reactions that result from the three mobile operators.
The question of whether it's really a coincidence, it's offered on its own.
It is the same as with the price of gasoline at gas stations because it is clear that it is the work of chance and a tough fight for competition.
"The Office in this regard has found no signs of unfavorable competition and therefore does not have reason to intervene in any way.
Neither operator has a dominant position therefore does not have a corresponding increase of responsibility in their actions on the market.
The tools necessary to open the mobile telephony market to virtual operators, rather are lacking, as well as telecommunications regulators," Chalupa added.
The interpretation of the dominant position in the market is one of the many conflicting points.
Of course, there is an analysis of relevant markets drawn up by the "T" whereby telecommunications are from the point of view of commercial tendering a single market.
Of course, none of the Czech operators have a dominant position in this type of market.
However, some of the legal experts offer another vision - for a virtual operator to emerge it is always necessary to agree with the network administrator.
And the operator of the mobile telephony - with all its logic - has a monopoly on its network.
It must therefore necessarily be dominant in a particular segment.
And if this argument weren't accepted, at least for OHS it should be a cavewise warning that three mobile operators are strikingly in a similar way.
The current complaints of the interested parties (unillusioned by GTS Novera) show that in all the negotiations with the operators they clash with many similar impediments.
Locating the responsibility in the "T" is a fairly common trickery in the field of telecommunications from which OHS was washes its hands throughout this matter.
We've already met him in some similar cases.
Unfortunately recently, a new amendment to the telecommunications law was recently tabled for the extension of the competence of the Treaty in the field of commercial tenders that did not take effect, even though a number of specialists promised that the situation would improve.
This must be well known by the representatives of OS, but it is clear that their interests are elsewhere.
Everything's okay when nobody looks
At the the same time, there is unnecessally that something like a pseudo-virtual operator that on Czech mobile phone networks there are already a few.
They are companies that place their own prices on mobile phone services that, however, only one part are public.
Usually these are multi-level marketing companies.
Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and many others offer their own mobile phone services.
Usually only a few chosen get the price list of these companies.
The magic is that the company, with regard to the operator, acts as a classic business customer of it by commissioning a large amount of SIM cards.
As a Special Customer you will then receive special prices and other offers.
The problem is that the commercial conditions in all Czech mobile phone operators prohibit this type of resale.
"If there were cases such as those quoting citing a violation under the terms of a contract that could result in an end to collaboration with the given society," Martina Kemrová replied to the question about this problem.
But it is quite unlikely that such an activity carried out by mobile telephony operators would not reach responsible people.
Precisely in the case of the three above-mentioned companies it may be thousands of customers.
Neither of the operators are against this procedure.
With the activities of these companies they make unscath despicable profits and, at the same time - unlike genuine virtual operators - in no way threaten their own official marketing.
Miroslav Eepick warns us of the possible problems: "the law allows speculation of this kind, however, the services of this company are used only by a despondent percent of customers.
They, moreover, must accept that the SIM card of their phone does not be in their name so they can't absolutely dispose of it as a normal customer of the operators."
Preci Precisely this is what it should look like the Minimally weird.
There's also a new question for OHS.
If mobile phone operators enclose their eyes in this type of procedure, why do they block the virtual operator's input?
It is necessary to add that OHS has sufficient competence to discover these types of activities and if you are not going to want it should not be content with the explanations of the operators that they have no idea of this history and that their commercial conditions do not allow it.
When we'll no longer be the most expensive.
As has already been made clear in the text, the virtual operator &apos; s entry into the mobile telephony market resulted in a significant decrease in the prices of services.
At the same time, in virtually all countries where the virtual operator emerged, the classic operators more or less tried to protect themselves from this eruption.
The arguments used by them were practically all identical - too small, a market, eventually too saturated, problems with the quality of the service, etc.
In the most advanced countries, however, the regulatory bodies have intervened at the moment and in none of the cases defended the operators but their clients.
Already with formal measures or informal pressure they managed to get the first virtual operator to emerge and others began to emerge with it.
Unfortunately, the Czech Regulatory Body this whole case passes as if it were a burning nail.
From our experience we cannot fail to be impressed that the confusing of powers in these cases suits them.
But let's not despair.
If this behind-the-scenes information is correct and the virtual operator really gets to this land, then the Czech mobile phone market will probably start moving again.
If any of the operators let in any virtual mobile phone operator it will be difficult for OHS to remain inconvenience to the (discrimination) rejection (discrimination) of other interested parties.
And maybe we'll no longer call it more dear than in the rest of Europe.
Czech anthem has four new versions.
Choose the best
I listened to the four presentations of the new version of the official Czech anthem.
On Sunday, Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek on the day of the official Czech celebrations unveiled the new official footage of the Anthem "Who," my home.
The new anthem was filmed by the National Theater Orchestra under the direction of Ji'í Blohlávek.
There are four versions available - two versions that are Solos, one with a masculine voice and the other with female voice, one with Choir and the other instrumental.
The song is taken care of by Kateina Kn?ínková and Adam Plachetka.
Perpetrators of the anthem arrangements are Otakar Jeremiá and Jaroslav Krsek.
On the iHNed.cz server you can listen to the four versions.
He sees this national Czech chant as one of the most beautiful anthems.
