Prague's MHD takes us Wednesday with the chip for the first time
On Wednesday, October 1, 2008, it is written in the history of the Prague Transport Company the date on which the first passengers enter the metro, tram and bus with an e-key in their pockets rather than a paper ticket.
It can now be acquired by almost a hundred thousand people who own the Opencard chip card.
Passengers can purchase the 30-day, 90-day and annual bond (this shall be valid from 1 January 2009) and simultaneously load it on their c-chip card at the infocents and transport company stalls at Metro, Muzeum, M-stek, Nádra-í Hole-ovice, Andel, Roztyly, Let-any, Kobylisy, Rajská zahrada, Kaerov and Luka.
The bonds can already be purchased on the Internet from yesterday on the websites of the transport company, www.dpp.cz.
The service, until the afternoon, was used by more than four hundred people.
Those who begin to use the internet as a form of commerce can definitely begin to forget about ticket sales posts at subway stations, where until now they had to queue regularly to take out a normal ticket.
The bonus is purchased on the internet, and the Opencard card is recharged in one of the thirteen subsidiaries of the Municipal Library.
"In these places there is available to passengers a window with prepared personnel that helps those interested with the purchase on the Internet and also with the recharge of the bonus purchased on their card," said project spokesman Martin Opatrn .
Apart from the library, it will also be possible to recharge the e-blink at the Opencard on automatic vending machines found in some subway lobbies.
What's these vending machines like?
They're red, they're reminding us of a snapping-ticket machine and travelers will probably've already noticed them at Roztyly Message, Bud-jovická, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská.
"Now there's five in the city but by the end of the year there would have to be eighties," Opatnor added.
The bonus is controlled by the machine rather than the driver
The use of the machine, which will start to work tomorrow, is simple.
Simply enter the Opencard chip card and the above-paid bonus will be loaded only on it.
Ordinaries from tomorrow are valid not only in the capital but also in its surroundings.
In other words, on bypass trains and bus lines from numbers 300 to 499 used mainly by the inhabitants of the towns of Central Bohemia.
"In the trains attached to the integrated transport of Prague there are revisers equipped with a reading card that will help to confirm the validity of the time and area of the bonus," explained the control of the tickets, Filip Drápal of the Ropid organization that establishes the schedules of collective transport in Prague.
Bypass buses have their reader cards assembled at the driver's locker.
Upon entering it is necessary not only to show the card to the driver but it is also necessary to accompany it with the Opencard in the place destined for it on the side of the Fund.
With this, the driver controls the validity of the bonus.
"If the passenger needs to verify the validity of the vouchers there is nothing easier than asking the driver for this information," Drápal said.
Passengers who don't travel on bypass buses and don't remember when the bond's validity ends can go to vending machines where the cards are recharged.
Here, on the screen of the machine, you will be shown the information about the validity of your document.
