Sylvana and the secrets of the Bent?-Box
When the first day Sylvana Hiltrop, a 16-year-old girl, had to show up at Tachibana High School the girls started laughing: The German is totally "kawaii."
- What's that mean?
Sylvana knows Sleeve's Word - in a report on the exchanges she tells Japan.
"The first day at a school in Japan.
I'm feeling nervous.
Already before I went to school I was scared.
I live on the edge of Tokyo in the direction of Kawasaki and go by train 40 minutes to High School Tachibana.
Sieve I had to find your way, luckily I didn't get lost.
It seems that the beginning of the classes isn't fixed as rigorously as in my house in Lower Saxony.
Between eight o'clock in the morning the students come, at nine minus twenty a small meeting begins with the teachers, the 'home room'.
Before I started the class, I'd gotten to introduce myself.
Besides, I'mn't a tasteful person in the public, especially in the face of a class of 40 students.
Since they still have the school uniform I wore an old skirt from the mother of my host family and also a blouse, blue socks and black leather shoes.
That's how dressed I put myself in the pulpit and I tried to utter everything correctly: "Sayonara Wo She Sylvana....." - My name is Sylvana, I am from Germany and in a week it will be my birthday.
At the moment I was very happy because for two years I had already started studying this language.
All 31 girls in class have whispered, laughed and made signs and I've heard the word "kawaii."
I've already known her from my sleeves.
That's the survival vocabulary of Japanese girls and it means "monkey, nice".
Then I slipped quickly in my place.
The girl next door had a big smile, for her it was an honor that the exchange girl had to sit next to her.
The rest of the class was quite quiet, but when the gong sounded, I really felt muzzled.
They all spoke at the same time, laughed and asked a lot of questions - at the same time it's very difficult to understand Japanese English.
Later in the recess everyone pulled out their Bentos.
"The mother of my host family also made a Lanchbox for me - it's something other than boring chunks of German bread.
It had rice with herbs, small sausages, cucumber salad and algae, fruit and something like tofu dough flesh.
In most cases I have no clue about how I am here in Japan.
Things are weird and have a new flavor.
What's certain is that it's all very wealthy.
Well, maybe with the exception of prunes marinated in the salt, bufff.
In Japanese schools blesses aren't just a luncheon.
Sometimes it happens that a girl prepares a bento made in very artistic mode for a kid and gives it to him in the hope of being able to know him better during a recess.
That's what I've read in my Manga-Comics.
I'm very curious to see if I can see him here too.
"Why precisely Japan?" my friends have asked me when I told them that I wanted to go to Tokyo to attend the school for a year.
Then I'd always answer:
"Well, because I'm fascinated".
I've finally wanted to read Mangas for years.
In order to better understand the comics and the culture, one day I began to study a little Japanese.
For me it was absolutely certain that one day I'm going to travel there.
Also because I've got a thirsty for tradition and customs.
They have great value here.
On the one hand Japan is a country of the latest technologies and trends, on the other hand it is a stern, disciplined and traditional country.
Since everything's well-organized, I haven't felt lost here yet although Tokyo is the biggest city I've ever seen in my life.'
The rats also have feelings.
A rat or cockroach is said to be feeling the danger flees, why is it also scary?
Joseph Le Doux, scient scientist of neurology is convinced that many animals also have feelings.
How can it be controlled if they, behaving in the way similar to us, feel the same thing?
An Internet magazine called "Edge" for many years has published the contributions of celebrity scientists.
SPIEGEL ONLINE presents in a series the answers chosen to the question What do you think is true without being able to prove it?
I think the animals have sensations and other states of consciousness without me or someone else being able to prove it so far, although we can't even prove that other people have consciousness.
But in this case we can at least expect it because all human brains present the same fundamental configuration.
"But as soon as we dedicate ourselves to other species and include questions about sensation and in general about consciousness, we go into the unknown realm because the hardware doesn't match.
When a rat finds itself in jeopardy, it does the same as the other animals: it becomes the dead, flees or attacks.
People be behave in a similar way, from which some scientists make conclusions about comparable subjective experience.
Of course I don't think so.
Two aspects of the brain structure make it difficult to spread our subjective experience to that of the animals.
First, they almost always include circuits associated with the human consciousness of the lateral prefrontal lobe of the cerebral cortex (because of their participation in short memory and executive functions that direct behavior).
This large part is much more developed in the case of people than the other primates and seems to be absolutely lacking in other creatures.
Bearing in mind at least the aspects relating to our consciousness that depends on the pre-frontal lobe of the cerebral cortex, among them knowledge about who we are and the ability to plan and decide, there's a reason to suppose that even man differs from other primates.
An other peculiarity is found in the linguistic nature of man: Since most of his experience is united with the tongue, it often means that consciousness depends on it.
In this case the animals can be excluded.
But even if consciousness would not depend on the tongue, it would influence it in such a way that the consciousness of the animals, created at any hand, would in many cases differentiate itself from most of our states of consciousness.
That's why it's hard to explain how the consciousness of other animals works.
If that, as something interesting and subjective, doesn't even be measured because it has the special hardware, it doesn't be analyzed through our experiences, so your research presents us with big problems.
Most of my interventions refer to the content of the conspicuous experience, but at the same time there is also another aspect of consciousness that would have to be easier for scientific research.
You could investigate the consciousness processes of animals without taking into account their contents, and that's what's going on during cousin short memory studies, especially in the example of visualization.
But with this approach followed by Christof Koch and Francis Crick, the essentials pass rather to the neutral correlates of consciousness than to the causal mechanisms: Both may or may not be similar.
Interestingly, this approach underscores the importance of the pre-frontal lobe of the cerebral cortex for the formation of visualization.
