Others have hailed Extra Place as a weird, virtually unchandted piece, a city raft that should be mentored.
Jeremías Moss, who directs a blog called Jeremiah's Vanishing New York (Subtitle, "The Book of Lamentations: A Bitterly Nostalgic Look at a City in the Process of Going Extinct"), has made available part of a talk of the city's history about Extra Place, published by The New Yorker newspaper in 1952.
Author Brendan Gill wrote: "Extra Place is a somewhat narrow and dead-of-the-cuff alley, also obscure for the day and marked by rusty-and-neck iron warehouse gates, with old-of-the-week-old newspapers blew through the gutters."
With few exceptions, the street seemed more or less the same on a recent afternoon.
A rat was cured through the back of the street and stood near the door that was used to enter the CBGB and now houses a luxurywear store.
Graffiti on the walls and an empty beer bottle of about 22-oz were close to a new batch of discarded newspapers.
Some interns said they rarely paid attention at Extra Place, but they suggested it could be cleaned.
Mr. Fields, meanwhile, noted that some people underestimate the attractive power of the street in its current state.
"I've spent the best years of my life," he said, "in obscure, wet and disgusting places."
The debate about bilingualism: immersion in the English language
