Lance T. Izumi, a long-time associate in California studies and director of studies at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, co-author of the book "Not As Good as You Think: Why the Middle Class Needs School Choice."
Effectively calling Hispanic voters is a tough business. It's a tough business.
Barack Obama &apos; s proposals on education are an example of that.
Mr. Obama &apos; s campaign notes that "African-Americans and Latino students are significantly less likely to graduate than white students," and this is very true consideration.
To combat such differences in learning events, Obama's education plan specifically advocates, inter alia, "cribling-transitional education" for English-preliving students.
However, the open question for Mr. Obama is whether the commitment to bilingual education, which prefers classroom instruction in languages other than English, is effective in nullifying the distance in the final results.
Take, for example, Sixth Street Prep, an officially recognized elementary school in the eastern region of Los Angeles County.
At this school, the vast majority of students are Hispanic and low-income families:
More than a third of the students, many of them newcomers, are learning the English language.
However, an incredible 100 per per cent of fourth-year-old students have been positively evaluated in the 2008 mathematical exam
And almost an equally staggering 93 per cent has dominated the English language and arts test.
This unbelievable success has been achieved using a different ingredient than Mr. Obama's favorite.
