In addition, this is where Mr. Obama should take note, according to Linda Mikels, head of the Sixth Street, the school's approach to educating English to students lies in "total immersion."
Immersion in English means the almost exclusive use of the language in the treatment of the contents of the instruction.
Mrs. Mikels, who opposes bilingual education, told me, "We've had extraordinary successes with students like the latter who's arrived from Mexico and that, if you go into the classroom 12 months later you wouldn't be able to collect what it was."
"It works," he said, "we think it'll work."
Will Mr. Obama take a school such as Sixth Street Prep as a model of replicating for other schools with large populations of Hispanic-English students?
This school's results should remove any doubts about the response, but his party's policy on the issue of education (the National Education Associations have long been advocates for bilingual education) and its very constant support for obscure Bilingual education the prediction of Mr. Obama's response.
While it's that immigrants must learn English, Mr. Obama has recently trivialized the issue by letting people stop worrying about the legislation that is only written in English.
And instead, she said, "you have to make sure that your kid can speak Spanish."
If Mr. Obama truly wants to improve the system and close loopholes, he should carefully consider the models of education that work well, rather than despise them or trivialize them.
The price of oil plumes sharply after renewed economic fears
The price of oil fell sharply on Monday due to concerns that the U.S. bailout plan for financial markets may not be able to revive the economy.
Crude oil shares fell 7 per cent to $US99.80 a barrel in morning trading on the New York Mercantile Exchange.
The price has fallen by more than $20 per barrel since last Monday.
Over the past two weeks, commodity markets have been buoyed by Wall Street's financial turmoil and a pair of mighty hurricanes in the Gulf of Mexico.
After peaking at $145.29 per barrel in July, prices had fallen to almost $90 per barrel earlier this month, before reversing, once again, because of huge uncertainty in the financial markets.
Anxiety gripped investors once again on Monday, despite congressional leaders who said they had reached an agreement over the weekend on the bailout plan, the largest in history.
The news was overshadowed by new worries that the financial crisis was far from over.
The evolution that occurred in the last episode of the merger process confirms that Citigroup will buy the Wachovia Corporation's banking operations, according to what the government said on Monday.
In the meantime, the Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments have partially nationalised the European financial conglomerate Fortis.
Analysts at Barclays Capital said at the frantic weekend the negotiations leading up to the bailout deal "suffering to reactivate market sentiment."
As the economic situation deteriorates, it is erupted in lower demand for commodities, including oil.
"The prospects for global equity, interest rates and exchange rates are going to become increasingly uncertain," Deutsche Bank analysts wrote in a note to investors.
"We believe that commodities will not be able to escape this contagion.
From the perspective of the products, our most pressing concern is the evaluation if the American virus will spread globally and specifically to China."
Analysts of the bank have lowered their expectations for the coming year, such as low oil consumption due to the crisis in economic growth and the forecast to reduce the price of oil and gas is 20 per cent by 2009.
