In a separate city, pride and the trials of life of color
Hidden by the park's sprawling of the great Orlando, a few kilometers from the brilliant, noisy and colossal city, there's a quiet town, where the pride and complications of the African-American experience become very evident.
Eatonville, the first city completely inhabited by built-in blacks in Zora Neale Hurston's country and childhood, is no longer as simple as she described it in 1935: "the city of five lakes, three croquet fields, 300 men of color, 300 good swimmers, many guavas, two schools and not a prison."
It's a pilgrimage place now.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to the annual Zora Fair in Eatonville to pay homage to Mrs. Hurston, Harlem Renaissance's most renaissance writer.
And yet, in many ways, the city she described - also making it a tour stage through its inclusion in the Florida travel guide produced by the Federal Writers of the Depression Season Federal Writers Project - it is still a separate place.
The city becomes independent, dignified and private as it was in the 1930s, when Mrs Hurston wrote that rural black people in Florida are often reluctant to share their true ideas with the white man, who "know so little about us and doesn't know what he's missing."
Even now, in the year when a black presidential nominee, senator Barack Obama, has drawn attention to begin a dialogue on the issue of race, many remain wary in front of the outsider.
"We're very wary about the way our history is told," said Jones Hortense, 59, a lifelong resident and member of the oldest church in the city.
"It's got to be right".
Eatonville has always been defined a paradox of triumph and wrestling.
At the same time is a historical model of the affirmation of Black people and a community of nearly 2,400 people living below a poverty level twice the national average.
It's a literary center, but it's also a restorative example of the rural culture of the black South - sometimes disdained, sometimes lauded - that was born out of slavery in America.
Unsurprisingly, if those who live here are both proud and reserved.
And the concern for Eatonville's image really began with Zora, as no one here calls him Mrs Hurston.
She introduced her hometown to the world through her deep dialect books such as "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God" (1937).
Five-episodes in the Florida guide transformed the city, barely outside Route 17, a road that runs through downtown Florida often forgo forgotten the history of blacks and human drama.
Facing so much in life and in literature, Ms. Hurston took her readers to Eatonville's first-teacher-owned store, Joe Clarke, and then inside more private areas.
"Out of the road to the left," he wrote, "there's the modern public mare school with its white finishes, well-kept jardines and play yard, cared for by Howard Miller, as he can barely read and write."
"She also mentions widow Dash's new husband and writes that Glenn Lee "sells drinks of all kinds and anything goes away with transitional rooms."
Therefore, in just a few hundred words, Ms. Hurston relates Eatonville with self-government, but also to illiteracy, second marriage and sex.
Evidently this Fodor didn't.
In fact, not everyone appreciated the portrait.
"Zora described it as far as it was," said Ella Dinkins, 90, one of the Johnson girls who immortamented Mrs. Hurston by quoting men who were singing colourless songs about their beauty.
She added: "Some people didn't like that."
Mrs. Hurston is still remembered here as an eccentric vivacious who returned frequently after her family moved to Jacksonville, in Florida.
Franklin Augusto, 77, is reminded that when Mrs Hurston arrived in the city, she did so unannounced in a large Chevrolet, smoking and wearing trousers in a city she still prides herself on glimpses of glimpses glimpse of glimpses of glimpsing.
"Most residents became fascinated," Mr. Franklin said, as many laughed sarcastically.
"People were always very happy to see Zora," Mr. Franklin continues.
But, he adds, swinging in his chair in a backyard overlooking Lake Sabelia, where Ms. Hurston was probably christened, "she never stayed too long."
When Mrs Hurston died in 1960, she was poor and her books were no longer in print.
Like most in the world, Eatonville also seemed to have forgotten about it.
Although she was a literary star, a Langston Hughes contemporary and the only woman of color at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was buried in an anonymous grave in Fort Pierce - Florida, where she had been alive.
In Eatonville, there were no great commemorative ceremonies, no great public readings.
"I don't think their contribution to the world or their inheritance was understood," said Valerie Boyd, author of "Wrapped in Rainbows," a biography of Ms. Hurston published in 2003.
A certain twist came in the 80s.
Orange County officials wanted to put a five-lane highway across the city to replace Kennedy Boulevard, the two-lanteath of the labor community.
Expansion of the Orlando area had already pushed Interstate 4 through the city's western edge.
The proposal came at a time when Eatonville was recovering from a tough stint in its history.
Forcible integration, among other things, had ended it relatively idyllic isolation from the community.
In the 1950s, the struggle for racial mixing brought hate closer to the community.
"Throughout that season, a bunch of white guys were walking around the city throwing oranges and everything against the people who were sitting next to each other," Mr. Franklin said.
"They also killed a girl, once.
They threw him at a watermelon of the car."
In a 1955 letter to Ms. Hurston he wrote to The Orlando Sentinel newspaper, criticizing Supreme Court's forced integration that in Brown v. Board of Education "obvious rather than honoring my race."
Remnants today say the de-segregation of schools, positive in some respects, had weakened Eatonville's cohesion and undermined the trust of its young people.
"Black kids were accustomed to being hugged - I'd remember this getting used to embracing the teacher in the morning and at night," said N.Y. Nathiri, Ella Dinkins' daughter and chief executive Preserve the Eatonville Community, a non-for-profit organization.
This lasted, he added, until the teachers and the students did not come from the same place.
"I wouldn't hug your teacher because it was white - there's a cultural gap there," Ms. Nathiri said.
The civil rights, however, helped to create the space for many else Zora Neale Hurstons - black writers, actors and performers who went beyond prejudice, just as she did, with buoyant, confidence and lines like: "How can anyone deny yourself the pleasure of my company.
I don't get it.'
Writer Alice Walker walked to Mrs Hurston's anonymous grave and began struggling to resurrect her reputation.
Five years later, an acclaimed biography of Ms. Hurston by Robert E. Hemenway appeared in the booksellers, reintroducing it to American canons.
The road project came just as Eatonville's most celebrity daughter went again under the reflectors.
Earlier this time Mrs. Hurston's old neighbours saw her as a savior.
In 1988, the community began planning a fair for Ms. Hurston to show what the county could splash with that road.
Thousands of admirers arrived at the inaugural event two years later, and every January, many return for the celebration.
After several years, the county has abandoned its road proposal.
"The resurrected idea of the five-lane road, it would have been putting in what you would call curvature velocity, true civic pride," Ms Nathiri said.
Mrs. Boyd put it more simply: "Zora saved Eatonville."
Victory over the road project has helped to change the image of the same city.
Out-of-town people such as Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who began coming to church here in 1993, began to appear in large numbers, and residents began reviewing the past.
Eatonville entered the National Historic Register in 1998.
A new library (carrying Hurston's name, of course) opened its doors in 2006, on a resurrected and embellished Kennedy Boulevard.
Nowadays, Eatonville is still an anomaly in Florida: just six miles from downtown Orlando, you can sometimes feel like on a street of the past under a summer rain, in a city as small as the one founded by just 27 black families in the 1880s.
(90 per cent of people are black today.)
Strasses who come in search of Eatonville's history of its meaning are often still treated with caution.
A permission in advance is necessary to do most of the interviews, and a few things - like murals in Eatonville's oldest church, painted by a white man and showing black men who work in the field - can't be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, such as Ms Jones, a brave and convinced teacher who has weakness for brilliant brilliant red, still fears her island community may be misinterpreted.
And yet as the Hurston festival has widened, a higher level of home pride has also sprung.
Young people, in particular, tend to see Eatonville in the same way Ms. Hurston saw their entire race: beauty, and all the problems, not better, not worse, and equally the other racial and ethnic groups in the Americas, proud, creative, hard-writing.
It's a sincere civic sense that can be heard in Mr. Franklin's nephew Edwin Harvey, 18-year-old Edwin Harvey, who is scheduled to return to Eatonville after university to work in local government or the Police Department, which he said might need some help.
And even those who are younger, such as Alondra and Alexia Kenon, two 11-year-old Winter Park twins, appear to have learned to describe Eatonville correctly.
"Most of the people, in case they dare go this far, they will have to think, 'Oh, this city is nothing compared to anyone else'," says Alondra Kenon after going to the church on a recent Sunday.
"But if you really stop and take a moment to look at its history, it's a very nice city."
This way, in New York, everyone talks about finance.
On the surface, nothing's changed.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from kiosks and gigantic television screens in Times Square, they wouldn't realize they were in the eye of the Hurricane.
They could never imagine that behind the glass facade of bee nest offices above their heads, bankers and speculative fund managers who monopolize New York are faced daily with the worst "wrong day of the office" since the 1929 bass.
Guys for home delivery keep evoking their metal carts for tea through the sidewalks and carrying trays of muffins for breakfast meetings.
Latino men continue to crawl between care apartment buildings, as well as human mules, with 50 eccentrating shirts hanging a yoke through their shoulders. Office workers are still queuing up for their morning-to-carry food, and it's still impossible to catch a taxi between 4 and 7 p.m. on working days.
Employees still queuing for their bagel to the vendors' carts, and it is still impossible to find a taxi between four and five o'clock in the afternoon days of the week.
It seemed to me as if there was no crisis at all, until this Thursday afternoon, with all the pessimistic forecasts, I have decided to open a new savings account.
Although I'd calculated how long it took for the operation to coincide with the late late-to-earth afternoon cool at my local JP Morgan Chase subsidiary, a queue of about 20 people was already expecting.
And not in front of the clerk's cleavage, to withdraw his cash or make a deposit, but in front of the customer service bank, where lifelong customers order their check heels, tourists are looking for instructions to go to Macy's department store and new customers want to open their bills.
"Most of these people preceded me, I was later informed by Starr, my "trusted personal benchman," and they were queuing for the same reason as me.
Contrary to me yet, they had already withdrawn their savings from the smallest banks in places from where they had commuted, places such as New Jersey, Pennsylvania and the Isle of Staten.
They'd pulled their money out of so-called savings associations (a bit like a construction company), the credit unions and the savings banks in their hometown whose bluffs only operate within the state of belonging, and who claim to treat customers like their family.
Over the past year, 15 of these banks have quietly failed.
In the last few days, Washington Mutual, a $327-billion-a-billion-year-old bankrupt, and as New Yorkers made their way to their homes on Friday night for the weekend, another bank, Wachovia, with $440bn savings deposits, was looking for a ransom buyer.
Workers in the offices and stores who were staying in front of me, fearing another collapse, were shifting their money to a bank to what is considered a safe haven, which is doing most of the bailouts on Wall Street.
Ordinary people in New York have in recent weeks become finance experts.
One woman in my local Chinese supermarket, speaking on the phone, was referring to Lehman Brothers, the failed bank, and its grieved-looking chief executive, Dick Fuld, commented on the matter familiarly as if they were complaining about a colleague's inactivity.
"And Lyn, and something a janitor in my apartment blob uniform, has changed the normal course of our conversations about Brad and Angelina or my shoppers to the shopkeeper.
He told me, after my visit to the bank, that "Now do I have all my savings in money market funds.
Commutation's done, I'm not taking any risks.'
Desecrated Muslim graves as Austria approaches the right
Heinz-Christian Strache, of the left wing of Austria's far-left Freedom party and Belgian Filip Dewinter-affiliated with far-right Vlaams Belang, take part in a demonstration to present the organization "Cities Against Islamization" in Antwerp on Thursday, January 17, 2008.
They wear traffic signs depicting the mosques with a red line coming through.
Police are blaming far-right extremists for desecrating a Muslim cemetery in Austria, on the same weekend that far-left political parties made huge profits in the country's general election.
More than 90 graves were severely damaged at Traun Cemetery, near Linz, at some point between Friday night and this morning in what police believe was an organised action.
Demonstrators spawned Jewish symbols, such as David's Star over some of the tombs, but investigators believe this may have happened because of the attempt to hide the real motives of hate-driven extremists against Muslim immigrants.
It's not clear if the incident was in any way related to the election.
A spokeswoman for the local Islamic community said they were "deeply shocked" by news of the desecration, which reached Muslim religious when the month of Ramadan ended.
Austria, meanwhwhile, is preparing for a round of introspection after turning right in yesterday's parliamentary elections.
Austria's two far-left parties that seized nearly 30 per cent of the vote, the Freedom Party and Austria's Alliance for the Future, had waged an energetic campaign in anti-immigration vehement and some of its slogans were considered xenophobic by critics.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the leader of the Freedom Party that won more than 18 per cent of the vote, campaigned against headscarves and burkas, and also voiced his opposition to foods deemed to be related to Islam.
At his last meeting in Vienna, he spoke of a "European Brotherhood" to prevent the emergence of Islam.
Both parties are trying to ban the construction of mosques and minarets, on the grounds that they were symbols of the on-going Islamisation policy in Austria and Europe.
Polls and analytics carried out in the immediate aftermath of the elections, which have confirmed the far-left as the country's toughest political bloc, indicate that the change occurred mainly by young voters who are concerned about their future in the European Union.
Abroad travel ban for Gary Glitter
The courts have banned Gary Glitter, the fascinating rock star guilty of child sexual abuse, from traveling to France and Spain.
Judges imposed a six-month ban on Glitter after he informed surveillance authorities that he wanted to travel to Spain through France.
Kent Police issued an overseas travel ban last Thursday after magistrates produced evidence of Glitter's previous convictions for child sexual abuse.
Glitter's real name Paul Gadd didn't attend the hearing at Ashford Magistrates Court in Kent but was represented by barrister Corker Binning.
A spokesman for the Court Service said: "Throughments at Ashford Magistrates' Court in Kent on Thursday, September 25, the police requested a provision to prevent Mr Paul Gadd's travelling abroad such as France and Spain until March 25.
"Paul Gadd didn't attend in person".
Glitter, 64, had informed the authorities that he wanted to travel abroad because he was under the sex offenders register.
He must inform the authorities if he wants to travel abroad for more than three days
and it is also necessary to communicate to the police the scheduled date seven days in advance of his departure.
Kenent police said they would not discuss Glitter's individual case or possible location in the county.
A Kent Police spokesman said: "Kent Police will do everything they can to protect children in this country, or abroad.
A Kent Police spokesman said: "Kent Police will do everything they can to protect children in this country, or abroad.
"From our point of view, it's easier to monitor someone who stays in the United Kingdom because here we have the systems to manage every potential risk more effectively.
"Protecting Kent's dwellers is the main concern of Kent Police and we are working closely together to monitor the behaviour of registered sex offenders in the county and to minimise any risks that may exist for the public."
Glitter returned to the United Kingdom last month after spending 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for the abuse of two girls, aged between 10 and 11.
When he was liberated Glitter flew to Bangkok where he took a flight to London.
However, he said he felt unwell and refused to fly to London.
U.S. authorities did not allow him to go through passport control and eventually flew to Hong Kong.
The Chinese authorities also denied his entry and he flew back to Bangkok, where Thai police and immigration officials insisted he would return to Britain.
He probably arrived at Heathrow's Terminal 3 on August 22 and stayed there while his solicitor David Corker went to Uxbridge Magistrates' Court in west London after Scotland Yard had successfully applied the order to register as a sex offender.
Glitter had been jailed in the United Kingdom for four months in 1999 for possessing child-abusing images.
An Interior Ministry source reported that Jacqui Smith, the interior minister, reassured and rejoiced to learn that Glitter had been banned from traveling to France and Spain.
"Get the off-scenes teacher to extract his character" Gonther Bauer-Photographer
Neither heels nor flashes nor premiership suits.
The throne of the great flamenco masters, stripped of the concentration in the interpretation to obtain the relaxed appearance of the man or the woman.
To one side the artist.
To the other, the person.
Unseparate except in intimacy, in those moments alien to the public, when to find the privacy of a gesture, the depth of a stolen gaze or the expressiveness of hands, carefree because they are not going to go on stage.
The German Guenther Bauer alternates in "Flamenco-people" the theatricality of some stagings with singers, dancers and guitarists with that less well-known side of these masters.
With this proposal, the photographer collects the particular iconography of flamenco from different fields.
Exclusive in the programming of Flamenco Photography Month, the exhibition "reflects the international importance of flamenco, greater every day," explains the creator, who clarifies that "Flamenco-people" "may be any people but, seeing the photos, the observer realizes that they are not the typical images of artists on the stage.
These people portrayed "on the street" are artists capable of conveying passion, sadness, rejoicing... captivating the audience.
To grasp the personality of these artists you don't have to take pictures of the stage.
They wear their personality and character inside.
What I was interested in was to create an intimate atmosphere that would allow me to grasp with the camera the personality of flamenco greats."
With the flavor of the "Cante de las Minas festival," Bauer began in 1,997 a long journey to portray singers, guitarists or dancers with a plaque chamber.
They are portraits that, due to the selection of the camera and the material, recall the classic photography.
The application of different stylistic media breaks with the classic base of the image, conferring modernity, an idea that reflects how Bauer himself understands flamenco: "Music with very traditional roots, which evolves and surprises every day."
It isn't the action of dancing, singing or playing in itself, but the personality of the artist, his character, that captivates with his art to the public.
The quest for an encounter with the essential, with the goblin that is made visible in a way of feeling, of being and of being are and have been the constant of this work that is presented these days in the gallery El Fotómata.
Known flamenco figures such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo, among others, make up this selection of almost seventy artists photographed.
The project has become an anthology of flamenco, which will soon be published in a book that will collect the portraits displayed in the exhibition.
Bauer's passion for portraits led him to repeat the idea two years ago with "Portraits of Veteran Athletes" ("SportARTproject").
Although the concepts are different in "Flamenco-people," the photographer admits that "I love portraying people, knowing them and being able to enter their lives for a moment, whether it's a long or short moment.
I don't think within photography portraiture is the toughest genre.
You find yourself or you're dealing with people, characters, who sometimes don't even know, or who leave you only a few minutes to take the picture.
In spite of these circumstances, as a photographer, I am always trying to realize my ideas."
This collection of portraits of the current flamenco entitled "Flamenco-people" is exhibited in the Gallery El Fotómata, located at the number 20 of Calle de la Mata, parallel to the Alameda de Hercules, where it can be visited until October 16.
Vargas Llosa confies that the novel he writes is "a recklessness"
It is set in places you don't yet know and it's about unfamiliar issues, but still, Mario Vargas Llosa is determined to carry out the new project in which he works, a novel that stars Roger Casemant, an Irish nationalists who lived twenty years in the Congo and went through the Amazon.
The always favorite to the Nobel Prize in Literature confessed yesterday in his passage through the Hay Festival Segovia that the project that today occupies him "is a recklessness" and that "everything indicates that he shouldn't write" the novel.
"If it was sensible, I wouldn't do it, but I'm gonna do it and I'm hoping to end it sometime," he concluded in his speech at the festival of the exchange of ideas, thought and words that for four days has seen a hundred writers, historians and journalists and thousands of people pass through the Aqueduct city.
The author of "The City and the Dogs" was one of the leading figures of the third edition of the word's feast and fulfilled the prognoses.
Vargas Llosa, who has won the Hay Festival award for being the writer most voted by the participants - an ancient edition of Dickens' "Our Mutual Friend" - filled the capacity of Juan Bravo the theater.
Presented by Félix Romeo, he found before him a dedicated audience to which he was made very short the time in which the Peruvian, with a prodigious memory and with great doses of humor, reviewed how his literary vocation was born in him, derived from the "extrordinary adventure" of "pleasure of reading" and "living more of what he lived"; how he writes a "disciplined manner" to supply his "lack of inspiration" with an intense work of documentation, correcting and reproducing a lot; how Faulkner, Víctor Hugo and Alejandro Dumas influenced their vocation and what "imgenres of memory" are the basis on which their stories are sustained.
The Spanish-American novelist acknowledged that "there is no way" to measure and to know if books change their lives, "but I refuse to believe that literature is pure entertainment, an ephemeral experience," stressed Vargas Llosa, who said it "has effects" and "makes people more dissatisfied," so he asked the writers "not to abandon themselves to frivolity" and to "suppose responsibly" their vocation.
