In his second bullfight, after a work of relief he earned the bronze of a large part of the audience.
They skip a checkpoint in Álava in a van with two thousand pounds of hashish
Five minus ten in the morning.
Traffic control of the Ertzaintza in the municipality of Armiñón (Álava).
The officers stop the two occupants of a van and they are fleeing.
Its cargo, more than two tons of hashish, wasn't enough to stop.
The officers persecuted them and arrested one of the two occupants, a 28-year-old male, identified as A. A.J., as reported in a statement by the Basque Government &apos; s Department of the Interior.
The undercarried individual has been charged with drug trafficking and falsification of a public document.
The incident occurred when several officers of the Alava Traffic Unit were carrying out a transport control on the N-1, as they passed through the municipality of Armiñón.
They were preparing to stop a van for inspection when the driver disobeyed the orders, rushed sharply and skipped control.
The officers began their chase until the vehicle stopped for a couple of kilometers later on the sidewalk, when its two occupants pulled up to flee the race through a wooded area.
The ertzainas finally reached one of them and seized the 2,178 kilograms of hashish they were carrying and found after inspecting the van, an Iveco Daily.
The rear of the vehicle was loaded with bundles of hashish, so they proceeded to transfer it to police units for a thorough inspection and weighing of the drug.
In addition, the officials found that the license plate had been "overwhelmed" because it did not coincide with the one collected in the documentation of the van, and the arrested person has also been charged an offence of falsificating public documents.
Ertzaintza is continuing the investigation to proceed with the arrest of the other suspect, who managed to escape the race as it has been said, and to determine the provenance of the apprehended drug.
The 28-year-year-old A.A.J. was transferred to police units to carry out appropriate proceedings and will be brought before the courts in the next few hours.
Actress Heather Locklear Arrested for Driving Under the Exacts of a Drug
Actress Heather Locklear, the Popular Amanda of the Melrose Place series, has been arrested this weekend in Santa Barbara (California) for driving under the effects of some medication.
A witness saw her perform bizarre maneuvers while attempting to get out of her car park in Montecito, a spokesman for the California highway patrol has told People magazine.
The witness has detailed that at around 4.30 p.m., Locklear "was treading the accelerator thoroughly, making a lot of noise, trying to get the car out of the carpark back and forth, and when he went back he passed several times over his sunglasses."
Shortly afterwards, the witness, who had apparently not recognized the actress at first, saw that Locklear stopped on a nearby motorway and got out of the vehicle.
It was this person who alerted the emergency services because "he was concerned about Locklear's life."
When the police patrol arrived he found the actress seated inside her vehicle, which was partially blocking the road.
"She seemed disorientated," so the officers took her to the specialized drug and alcohol center for a test.
According to a police spokeswoman, the actress was cooperative and the excess of alcohol was discarded from the very beginning, although "as the officers observed at first glance, we believe that Locklear was under the influence of some medication."
Locklear has been arrested on a suspected feloe of driving under the effects of unprepared substance and entered the local prison at around 7 p.m. although she was released a few hours later. she was released a few hours later.
Two months ago, Locklear walked away from a treatment clinic in Arizona where he was recovering from an episode of anxiety and depression.
Llamazares urges the Andalusian federation of IU to assume its powers in the Silva case
The general coordinator of IU and congressman of the United Left, Gaspar Llamazares, said today that it will be the regional formation federation that will have to make a decision on the case of the former councillor of Youth and Sports of the City of Sevilla, Francisco Manuel Silva, who resigned last week after he was accused of carrying out deals in favor to relatives.
"For these things I am very federalist, and it will be the Andalusian organization that has to assume its competences," Llamazares said about the action that the party will have to adopt with the one council so far a councillor in the Sevillian consistory.
However, the IU leader defended that the need for "transparency" to be present in the possible investigations into the irregularities Silva could have committed: "IU has always defended that these types of issues have the maximum degree of transparency."
In the same vein he stressed that the formation has "strict ethical codes for all public officials."
'Blood of May', Garci's epic bet
Inspired by the National Episodes of Benito Pérez Galdós, José Luis Garci recreates the famous uprising of the Spaniards against the French in Sangre of May, which is the most ambitious film in his filmography with about 15 million euros of budget.
In this way, the filmmaker has tried to "illur the torrent of characters and social analysis" that he has always admired in the work of the well-known novelist, he explained today during the presentation of the tape, with Quim Gutiérrez and Paula Echevarría at the head of a wide cast of performers.
Blob of May, with a footage of more than two-and-a-half hours, premieres this Friday in theatrical cinemas, to become a miniseries for Telemadrid of more than four hours.
Great admirer of Galdós Literature, José Luis Garci claims to have fulfilled this shoot "a dream of childhood," who should thank Esperanza Aguirre, who from the Community of Madrid commissioned him three years ago to shoot a commemorative tape for the second centenary of May 2, Madrid, something he accepted as long as the story was based on the National Episodes.
The 15 million budget provided in its entirety by the Community of Madrid constitute for Garci a "unique opportunity" to set with the decorations and the appropriate costume the portrait of "an atlonded Spain who was mistaken as boss to fight, since the struggle for independence led the people to be very late with the reign of Ferdinand VII," he said.
But the filmmaker regrets that he did not have a funding such as Alatriste or The Ghosts of Goya - more than twice that of Sangre de mayo - so he has not been able to shoot some scenes reflected in a script signed with Horacio Valcárcel, with whom he has collaborated in almost his entire career.
For this reason, the director of Back to Begin, has emphasized in what is his usual form of work, the staging with the actors, with whom he usually rehearses before starting the shooter and those who do not casting, because he seems "humiliating" and considering that "part of the director's trade lies in going to theater and the cinema to know what is happening in the industry in which he works."
Many of the performers with whom he coincides in Sangre in May repeat before his camera, such as Miguel Rellán, of Carlos Larrañaga or Fernando Guillén Cuervo.
Candidate of the Oscar on four occasions - of which he won one - Garci considers that any of the three candidates to represent Spain this year before the Hollywood Academy, the Blood of May itself, Seven French billiard tables and the finally selected, The Blind sunflowers, do not represent a young generation of Spanish cinema, "which is the one who must make themselves known," he said.
Garci doesn't expect awards with this film.
"At the beginning you think you work for posterity, then you realize that you only work for prosperity, and now you settle for making one more film," Garci explains after more than 30 years of professional career.
Isabel Bayón recovers the essence of the Sevillian dancer Tórtola Valencia
A Sevillian dancer will pay tribute to a Sevillian dancer.
Carmen Tórtola Valencia, of the Catalan Father (Floren-Tórtola Ferrer) and an Andalusian mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in the Triana neighborhood and when she was three she moved to London with her family.
She was a free dance dancer and specialist in oriental dancing.
He had an exciting life, a pioneer of women's liberation, such as Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
He was a Buddhist and vegetarian and called for the abolition of the corset that impeded women's free movement.
The life of this woman who marked a dance and aesthetic revolution in Europe between wars fascinated Isabel Bayón.
"I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa and she proposed this character and I found it incredible."
According to the dancer, "we don't pretend to tell your life but to collect your spirit.
The titles of his dances have suggested flamenco sticks.
Thus, "the dance of the butterfly" suggested to me a guajira, "the dance of the snake" some tangos.
His dancing was sensual and I've been told that my dancing is within this aesthetic as well," he says.
The montage has two exceptional collaborations: Miguel Poveda who will re-sing Elizabeth in one in his shows, just as he did in that of "The Open Door"; and with teacher Matilde Coral, who exceptionally has wanted to collaborate with who was his student.
"Matilde's Mossing of Elevity, the character of maturity.
I'm Happy 'because I've agreed to perform in my work and I'm very honoured by it.'
The montage also has the musical composition and guitars of Jesús Torres and Paco Arriaga, the singing of Miguel Ortega, the Pulga and Moi de Morón; the percussion of El Pájaro, as well as Rafa El Viola's viola and the collaboration of an actor, Juanfra Juárez, "who plays the role of the eternal companion of this type of artists who were divas in its entirety, and who are always in love with them."
The work also tells in any way the life of this woman that developed in places so far away, especially for the time, such as India, the United States, or Russia, in addition to the whole of Europe, since she usually resided in London.
"I've wanted to remember it because I think it's from those women who have an enormous influence at a certain time and now nobody knows and much less values their work," Bayón says.
The spectacle will kick-start today at 8.30 p.m. at the Lope de Vega theater.
The publication of the "Diary of a New Married Poet" also closes the Triennium
The presentation of the first enlightened edition of the "Diario de una Poeta Reciencasado" in Saint Stephen's Church (New York), where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in March 1916, is framed among the activities programmed by the Ministry of Culture of the Junta de Andalucía to celebrate "Trienio Zenobia and Juan Ramón" (2006-2008), organized in order to commemorate the fiftieth anniversary of the award of the Nobel Laureate of Literature and the death of both.
In this way, "Trienio Zenobia and Juan Ramón" comes to an end with the reissue of this work, with the International Congress that has been celebrated in New York, with the Restoration of the Nobel Museum House and with the itinerant exhibition "Juan Ramón Jiménez: The Chopo of Light," which was inaugurated last May and which, after traveling through the different Andalusian provinces, it is planned to travel to New York during the course of the International Congress.
Likewise, the Ministry of Culture of the Junta de Andalucía has acquired a total of eight hundred copies of the "Diario de una Poeta Reciencasado," edited by Pedro Tabernero and illustrated by Jacobo Pérez-Enciso, in order to continue the dissemination of the figure and the work of one of the most universal Andalusian poets.
America likes it least without Paul Newman
"Sometimes God Creates Perfect Persons.
Paul Newman was one of them".
The phrase is by actress Sally Field, who worked with him on "Absence of malice," and gives the idea of the helplessness of American cinema, and from all of America, to the shocking idea that Newman is gone.
Look, they had a hard time giving him an Oscar.
And he's been retired since 2007.
And it was known that he was very ill.
It's still been a dungeon for everyone.
"Sometimes what you feel is beyond every expression, and now I've lost a great friend," Robert Redford cried, "my life and my country were better thanks to their existence."
No one was scared to make a fool of themselves or exaggerate.
There had been the death of a father of cinema and homeland at a particularly dark time, with fear of terrorists and markets, with American self-esteem on the ground, desperately seeking reasons for dignity.
When so tough it's time and without virtue men and very few beings to admire, you don't need to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
It incarnated an almost platonic ideal for everyone of beauty and truth.
He was Richard Nixon's formal enemy and an advocate of loss-and-earned causes from the drug-fighting foundation created in honor of his first-born death for overdoses to his home-grown and organic food brand.
Quite a gift to a country it's had a hard time to overcome a suicidal food culture.
The expansive ripple of admiration swept Hollywood without attending to nationalities or passports.
The last James Bond, Englishman Daniel Craig, noted that a man has not died but an era.
Another Englishwoman, staunch director Sam Mendes, said working with Newman was nothing more or less than the peak of his career.
Australia's ill-hundurty Russell Crowe dared to call Newman his greatest inspiration.
"It's left the bar very high for all of us," said George Clooney, perhaps one of the few who today dares to try to live as Paul Newman lived: keeping the demand for probity loud and having a serious private life away from the cameras.
This isn't always easy or even possible in today's media jungle.
But there's someone who's trying, and Paul Newman is his example.
He became divorced once but was later married to Joanne Woodward 50 years, to whom he was faithful when there were surely plenty of chances of not doing so.
"But why am I going to eat hamburger out of the house, if I have a steak inside?" he used to joke himself.
In the sky he is given the pride that on the earth his daughters coincide with his fans, something not so common in America.
"From all the great roles he played, none of whom were as proud as that of devout husband, loving father, solicitous grandfather and dedicated philanthropist," they say.
The Imperfect Race of an Olympic Madrid
Yeah, the dream of many of us is that some Olympics will be celebrated in Madrid and, after the failed 2012, it may be 2016.
"But I'm wondering, are we ready?"
If we follow yesterday's race's example, I'm saying no.
The perfect race was called, and in my opinion it's been the worst race I've ever been.
A height of nonsense that, of course, if you took it into account as an evaluation and I examined, would be a very clear suspense.
But let's go in parts and from the beginning.
The nonsense begins with the collection of the dorsal, which makes you have to go first.
I don't think it's too bad, but if they're already making you give everything (including the chip to avoid queues in the race).
Well, don't.
Here they only made you leave to collect the dorsal (first nonsense, go for that only) and (behind the relevant tail), Surprisy, there was only size 'S' for the T-shirt (second nonsense, because I need an XL size)
Which world do we live in?
Is it that because of the crisis you have decided to buy only small T-shirts with fewer fabrics and cheaper?
Well, things like this are a minutiae compared to those of the day of the race.
Apart from having to go too soon to pick up the chip, the tail when you arrive is 15 minutes and, in addition, you have to leave the backpack.
