I don't think about 500 people will surely have pneumonia tomorrow, and some like me won't even appear with the right time in the ranking, because of the chip they gave me that, I imagine, it didn't mark my exit correctly.
Of course, if they asked me about the perfect race, it wouldn't go into my thoughts.
So I think we're going to keep dreaming of a well-prepared Olympian city.
Microsoft seeks receptionist (virtual)
MIT's Emerging Technologies Conference has shown an advance of a new image recognition and human interaction system, a sort of Artificial Intelligence without a lot of intelligence but capable of performing some basic tasks, such as ordering a taxi (or in the case of Microsoft, a shuttle to move on the company's campus) according to what it hears in the conversation.
Thanks to a camera and a form-and-facing recognition system, this "receptionist" knows how many people are in the room, who they are - even to remember what they wear - and to hear and understand the key words of their conversations.
Also join those present according to the conversational groups.
In the sample video the receptionist detects a third person but understands that it does not belong to the group who are talking to her and who is not an employee of the company.
He asks him if he's there to visit somebody and tells him he'll take care of him soon.
It's all unflagged blue.
Impressive.
But that, of course, is the future.
Now the company has better things to concentrate on, for example the next version of its operating system, Windows.
This week there's been much talk about a change of strategy at Microsoft this week.
Windows 7 will be lighter and you won't have so many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer has discovered that if the computers came with the essentials rather than commercial bass loaded, they'd go five times quicker - man, Steve, it'll already be for less.
An entire finding taking into account that Microsoft, version after version, has added features and "extras" to Windows.
The change of course is appreciated.
Microsfot is right, the operating system is losing importance, as well as the software.
When Google Chrome came to the net, I was one of those who joined the theory that it was Google's first step to compete with Windows.
Many rightly pointed out that a browser isn't an operating system.
The operating system will always be necessary because it is the essential layer for the Hardware and the Software to be understood.
Fulfill the same function as your wife or your husband when you have to say something to your mother-in-in-law.
That's right.
It will never go away, but you have to think about the "death" of the operating system not as an absolute truth, but relative.
What Ballmer has said this week is confirmation of this idea.
People pay less and less attention to the operating system, and more to the web browser.
The computer boots only to execute the Firefox or the Explorer - or Chrome - and every day we spend more time navigating than in the window environment.
What's the use of half of the applications that come in the system?
Less is more.
And Windows 7 needs to be less, much less, than Vista.
Less heavier, less recharged, less complex.
Microsoft is more interested in integrating its online services and ultimately being an alternative to Google.
You desperately need it, in fact, and I've got a little example.
The photo of this post was taken in one of Microsoft's employee rest rooms in Redmond earlier this month.
On the soda machine a poster asks you to use your search tool - "Your Search. Our Future."
In 2006, an independent study concluded that 66 per cent of Microsoft's employees used Google to search the network.
Last last week, by the way, a delay was also confirmed in the new version of Windows Mobile 7.
It won't arrive at the beginning of 2009 but at the end, rather pulling to 2010.
In Microsoft they will surely be searching for the joint release of Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7.
The phone, after all else, is more and more computers every day.
But the delay, with Android and the iPhone already on the street, is going to be billed.
Le Fabuleux Marcel
Pablo Picasso wore it in the Cote d'Azur, Leonardo di Caprio wore one in Edward Zwick's 'Blood Spharm', as well as Al Pacino in Francis Ford Coppola's 'The Godfather' and is part of the uniform of the musulated gay parish.
