1001, the anti-stress number
There's not 1000 but 1000 plus a way of forgetting the chaotic and frantic daily life.
As if that weren't enough, they're all assembled in a book that will bring relaxation to the bookstores on September 30.
Awakener almost punishes when it rings every morning at 7 a.m., but it's even worse to imagine wrangling wrapped in the hectic rhythm of today's life.
The office's called tension and computer back pain.
Stress is 21st-century evil.
But there's not one or two methods to combat daily exhaustion and disengagement problems with the world of work.
- No, the number amounts to, nothing more and nothing less, than 1001.
Regardless of yoga positions, the classic massage (spa', Susannah Marriott gathers in the pages of a book an entire 'menu' of possibilities adapted to the taste and minutes of each.
The anti-stress work is called '1001 Ways to Relapse' and its cooler will reach the booksellers by Pearson Alhambra and DK publisher.
If someone considers that it is not possible to achieve tranquillity in areas such as work or through personal relationships, this 'manual' denies such a belief.
In addition, it offers other procedures such as being in contact with nature or 100% natural recipes that contribute to relaxation.
If the adrenaline no longer runs through your veins to restore the balance of the body and stress has become a problem, it's time to sit on the sofa, disconnect your mind and focus on reading.
Sleep.
Some kids talk at night.
I don't mean those who wake up very squeezy by telling us something that happened the day before, but to children who are talking asleep.
The first time it'll happen maybe you'll take a scare, but it doesn't really matter.
It's a benign sleep disturbance that's called somilochios.
It's quite common, especially in children and teens.
I'm sure more than one has been told he's talked at night.
This phenomenon can appear very sporadically (once in the life for example associated with an episode of high fever), or on a more regular basis (every day, once a week, once in meseS...), the next day the child does not remember anything.
There's no single cause that determines its appearance.
They are often linked to other sleep disturbances such as nightmares, nocturnal terrors, nocturnal enuresis (sleep-sleeping enuresis) or sleepwalking, but it doesn't have to be so.
As we have already mentioned above, they can also be associated with very high-floors, stress or overexamination.
Either way it's an unstudied phenomenon.
The intensity with which it appears is also very variable.
You may speak in a quiet tone of voice, loose-worst words, a murmur or the opposite (screams, cry, long speeches...).
In the cases in which the demonstration is very intense it would be advisable to look at the child and consult with a specialist, to see if there are any issues that is generating a lot of anxiety and gives him a way out in this way.
We've said it's a benign alteration, so you don't have treatment, unless it's associated with another sleep disturbance that can be treated (e.g. if it's linked to sleep apnea or a stress picture).
What can be done is to take into account a number of very simple preventive measures such as:
Try not to excite the kid very much before they go to sleep.
So look for quiet games and avoid programs or television series that are scary or a little violent.
With very nervous children who find it very difficult to relax, relaxation techniques can be used to help them acquire a certain inner calm.
This will make it easier for the child to have a quieter sleep.
Once somniloquies appear, try to stay calm.
Remember that they are harmless to the child and that the next day you won't remember any of what's happened.
Useful Tips
Don't awaken him.
It's useless, 'cause he's unconscious, you're gonna tear up his dream.
Even if he's talking, he's asleep and he's thus resting.
Remember that if you are very distressed during several episodes you should consult or find out what generates so much anxiety in the child.
If you understand what you say (which isn't the most common), don't fall into the temptation to interrogate you to learn.
You won't get anything, 'because being asleep isn't conscious enough to organize your thinking.
In addition you will hear what it says, but you don't know what you're teasing you're teasing to say that, taking great care of how you interpret the information and the conclusions you draw.
Don't tell the kid that you can question him at night to find his secrets.
Don't even joke.
That generates a lot of anxiety.
In short, don't worry, it's not bad, it's an episode that lasts only a few minutes.
