Sarkozy stunner finds angry fishermen
Before taking off to the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy will take a swirl through Brittany.
Faced with the demonstration of resistance on the part of the fishermen who strongly disturbed last Monday the entrances of several Breton and Norman ports, the President of the Republic decided to go last Tuesday morning to the port of Guilvinec, in the Finistare, to find their representatives there.
They vowed to welcome him "in a constructive spirit" and hope that "he will come with concrete propositions ."
This presidential tour coincides with the general meeting of the "crisis committee" created by Breton fishermen to obtain measures in the face of the increase in diesel prices, which marks a record reaching 1.14 euros per litre.
Elysée spokeswoman David Martinon said the president "would discuss with them solutions that can be imagined to help their companies to become competitive again and to save seafarers' conditions of jobs and wages."
Fishermen await Nicolas Sarkozy on firm foot.
On Tuesday morning, they carried out operations to lessen the circulation in the Morbihan and Calvados, which have thus caused traffic jams of almost 10 kilometers.
Chiberon's harbor has also been blocked, but the connections to Belle-le haven't been stopped.
"A leaking barrier" has been placed on the Quiberon peninsula.
Sailors already on Monday showed their determination burning, scratching the dawn, tires and wooden pallets in front of the fuel depots of the ports of Brest, and Lorient, while demonstrations took place in Concarneau or Saint-Brieuc.
The movement was spread to other ports of the Atlantic Coast and La Mancha, such as Les Sables d'Olonne, in Vandea, where the Bous stayed in the harbor, or in Le Havre, where about twenty ships blocked the movements of commercial ships.
The fishermen of La Turballe and de Croisic, in the Loira-Atlantic region, stopped in the afternoon the blockade of the important Total refinery of Donges.
The fishermen of Boulogne-sur-Mer decided to wait until Wednesday's reunion that the ministry will take place to think about possible actions.
In Guilvinec, the "crisis committee" will have to think on Tuesday about the development of the movement and its participation in the meeting planned in the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
But some Breton fishermen don't want to be represented by leaders of the national fisheries committee, which did not sustain its movement.
"The strike will continue "because the fishermen did not quit" and "they do not want to travel to Paris," said Liliane Cariou, the chairman of Lorient's fisheries committee.
Occupational fishermen, who do not pay fees on diesel, currently pay about 50 cents per litre but consider that the financial equilibrium point of a ship can only be reached with diesel with a diesel price of less than EUR 0.30 per litre.
