The Brazilian builder relies heavily on the Chinese market particularly to sell planes ranging from 30 to 120 seats.
Today, Embraer delivered 41 gadgets to Chinese customers in eight years.
AIG would aim to sell more than 15 per cent of its assets
Insurer American International Group, which has been buoyed by U.S. monetary authorities, aims to sell more than 15 per cent of its assets to repay its $85-billion loan, according to Sunday's edition of the Financial Times.
AIG is poised to study the sale of most of its services beyond its international life insurance and retirement insurance in the United States, this newspaper says.
AIG's Board of Directors met last Sunday afternoon to examine potential sales, according to the Financial Times.
Neither confirmation could have been obtained from AIG.
AIG's assets aim to secede include its airline leasing subsidiary, International Lease Financial Corp., its 59 per cent stake in insurer Transatlantic Holdings as well as its real estate portfolio and private equity investments, the Financial Times adds.
"No final decision has ever been made on these sales," the Financial Times chases.
AIG must quickly carry out liquidity to repay its $85-billion loan granted by the Federal Reserve at the risk of settling the U.S. authorities taking 80 per cent of its capital, diluting it with due proportion among current shareholders.
