"In Minsk, " have you noticed the elections in Minsk?"
Young people and opponents demonstrated last Sunday afternoon in the main square of the Belarusian capital to reject in advance the legislative results.
Hundreds of Belarusians have gathered on Sunday afternoon since the closure of polling stations in Minsk's central square to protest against the fraudulent nature of the legislatures.
While no results had yet been announced, many young people had come to protest against the power of President Alexandre Loukachenko: "the dictator, he must resign ."
Some recognize that they did not vote: "why, since they will do what they want with my vote," says a young man.
They don't believe that the regime that put foreign observers right in order to renovate contacts with the European Union is prepared to bring about democratic change.
Penalties, which include visas for 40 Belarusian leaders and the freezing of certain assets, could be lifted if these elections are seen as fair for observers.
They weren't, according to the opposition.
"We have been placed next to the electoral commissions and our observers were unable to participate in counting the votes," lamented Iouri Potemkine, the spokesman of the Union of the Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous regroupment of twelve parties.
"It's a cosmetic change," says Alexandre Milinkievitch, a former opposition candidate for the 2006 presidential election and leader of the pro-European movement, because the important thing is who counts the votes, and in our house, is the executive branch.
If some of the Opposition MPs come into parliament, it is because they will have been appointed but unelected.
"But his entry into parliament can mean television appearances, meetings in the regions and bill proposals."
The opposition must therefore enter the political game because "we must start the dialogue with the European Union.
Because if our economy, which has a lot of debts in front of Russia, goes into crisis, and if we don't reform, we're in danger of becoming a protectorate of Russia.
"But we want to be independent."
The opposition fears that the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe that followed the electoral process will not recognize the validity of the vote.
He points out that the turnout figures appear to be too high, and that the generalization of the vote in advance (26 per cent of voters in Minsk) favored manipulations.
"Have you not noticed the elections? Introducing the Social Democratic leader Alexandre Kozouline, one of the last two political prisoners released in August this year.
There wasn't anyone in the polling stations.
And there'll be a top five MPs from the opposition to the parliament, who are better suited to power.'
The silence of the night returns again in the square of October.
On the television, President Loukachenko and members of the central electoral commission welcome their success.
Conversation - Blood Will Be Safe Again
Héma-Québec yesterday celebrated its 10th anniversary of its foundation, and its president, Francine Décary, was right to rejoice, because Héma-Québec has again been one of the safest and most effective suppliers of blood in the world.
And above all, it regained the confidence of the public who had seriously been hesitated by the contaminated blood scandal in the 1980s and 1990s.
As it is known, national blood reserves administered by the Canadian Red Cross Blood Transfusion Service have been contaminated by Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) and Hepatitis C Virus during the 1980s.
Faced with this drama and dissatisfaction of the public, the Government of Canada conducted in February 1994 a public inquiry, chaired by Judge Horacio Krever, which sought to clarify the circumstances that had led to such a tragedy and make recommendations so that such a disaster should no longer be replayed.
While Judge Krever took a long time to present their relationship, certain provinces then set up a committee that aimed to plan a new blood management system in Canada.
In Québec, the Gélineau committee fulfilled this mission.
As a result of the Gélineau and Krever reports, two providers have been set up in Canada: Héma-Québec to provide Qué Quebec and the Canadian Blood Society to supply all other provinces and both territories.
"In 1998, when Héma-Québec began its activities, almost 38% of people still had confidence in the blood system in Quebec," Decary recalls.
The situation was disheartening.
Having made a lot of effort to improve the security of blood products and a lot of work over the years that followed, we have regained public confidence," he says before proudly asserting that at the beginning of 2008, about 94 per cent of the Quebec population was hereinafter trusted in Héma-Québec.
Dr. Décary nevertheless recalls that "confidence is an extremely fragile thing."
Since its inception on September 28, 1998, Héma-Québec applied the precautionary principle.
At that time, we were witnessing in Europe the birth of numerous cases of the human variant of crazy cow disease, a new variant of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, says Decary.
And we didn't know if this disease could be transmitted through the blood.
Then we rely on one of the main recommendations of the Krever and Gélineau reports that when we don't know it and when a serious disease can emanate from it, in this case the Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease that is characterized by degeneration of the brain that inevitably leads to death, we establish exclusion measures.
In April 1999, we deny the blood of people who had spent more than a month in the United Kingdom since 1980.
But a few years ago it's been proven beyond doubt that this disease is transmitted by the blood.
"This episode thus showed us that the precautionary principle that was applied had the meaning."
In 1999, a much more sensitive technique of HIV detection appeared on the market: nucleid acid tests that make it possible to directly detect the presence of the virus in the blood of the donors the presence of the virus, on which the tests used so far were based.
Nucleid acid tests made it possible to shorten the period between the time the person has been infected and the time when the first viruses can be detected in the blood because of our techniques, the specialist explains.
We were able to reduce this period by a duration of less than a week.
This substantially reduces the risk that certain donors will be infected unknowingly and that we cannot detect them.
These enhancements significantly reduced the risk of getting sid AIDS from a blood transfusion.
Nowadays, one person every 12 million is at risk of receiving HIV-contaminated blood.
"That means that 12 million blood products will have to be distributed before someone is infected with HIV.
So since Héma-Québec distributes between 400,000 and 500,000 blood products each year, they will spend more than 24 years before a case of HIV transmission by a blood product occurs.
I think you can receive or prescribe a blood transfusion today to regain health without worries," says the head of Héma-Québec's management.
"Our challenge for the next decade is to maintain the safety of our products.
It's never known which pathogen might emerge in the system.
Last year the Chikungunya Virus appeared on Reunion Island, which, like the West Nile Virus, is transmitted by an insect in the blood.
You have to be constantly vigilant ," says Dr. Décary before stressing that in addition to a secure supply, Héma-Québec must also provide for a sufficient supply.
" Needs are increasing because the population is getting old.
Because more aggressive cancer treatments, patients often need an injection of red blood cells or platelets.
The ageing of the population also has an impact on the network of blood donors, which regroups on average only 3 per per cent of Québequeses eligible for blood donation."
All persons in good health, aged 18 and corresponding to the eligibility criteria, can nevertheless donate blood every 56 days or six times a year.
On an average of 14 per cent, 14 per per cent of people proposing their blood are unaccepted because they suffer from AIDS or malaria, for example, and if they travelled in a country where malaria reigns, they will be excluded for a period of one year.
"With this 3 %, we managed to maintain an inventory that covers the needs of the hospitals.
Phythages are very weird.
But members of this network of donors must be renovated as they get older and can no longer donate their blood.
But it's hard to persuade people to donate their blood, but above all retain them and encourage them to be a regular donor.
Since today's life is very unbridled, we rarely find the time to spend an hour to donate your blood ," Francine Décary grieves.
With a budget of $297 million a year, Héma-Québec supplies not only blood products, but also human tissues, including bony grafts, cardiac valves, eye balloons and skin that is almost exclusively used for large burns.
These woes are analyzed, processed and frozen before being stored in Hema-Québec, which also manages the only umbilical coat public blood bank in Quebec.
The umbilical cord that was formerly regarded as biomedical waste is filled with rich blood of stem cells that can be used to treat serious diseases such as leukemia.
"We have celebrated a few weeks ago, putting on the bench of our thousandth sample of umbilical cord blood and it is expected to remove more than a thousand of these per year.
We'd have to start proposing it for graft at the beginning of next year ," says Doctor Decary who describes this lace-blood-wielding program as very popular.
"Many moms are displeased they can't enjoy this ," since Héma-Québec doesn't have the resources to propose this complex and expensive program in all Quebec hospitals.
Softball - The Impact in the semi-finals
The Impact of Montreal was qualified for the semi-finals of USL's first-division elimination series, thanks to a 3-1 (4-3 victory in summary ends) at the expense of the Seattle Sounders, yesterday, during the second and decisive game of the quarter-final series, at Saputo Stadium.
