Charest takes care of the North.
The Prime Minister doesn't rule out starting elections by the end of the year
As soon as the General Council of the Liberal Party ends, Jean Charest was yesterday in Juan-Talon in Quebec, where the new Minister of Health, Yves Bolduc (left), tries to be elected during today's by-election.
Rejecting the bounds of our last great northern border": such is the long-running ambition that Jean Charest presented with visual force by staying in the broad lines, at the end of the general council of the Quebec Liberal Party in Lévis, Yesterday.
Behind him, during his speech, images were projected.
They paraded on three immense screens, spectacular images of hydroelectric reservoirs, of mining, of Quebec seen by "Google Maps," maps, etc.
Great means as during the electoral period.
The Prime Minister thinks the elections will take place this autumn, although he showed that his schedule was very full.
The "Clean Plan" is the fourth-largest side of a "vision" that the Prime Minister intends to have for Québec, that of a "new economic space," which includes an agreement on manpower with France, an alliance between Canada and Europe and harmonies with Ontario.
Frequently mentioned in Prime Minister &apos; s speeches recently, the opening of the North - the Northern Territory to Parallel - had never been the subject of a presentation.
The Prime Minister merely explained why the North was "a jewel to immense potential ," but did not give a term, amounts of investment or precise priority in future projects.
What could, above all, be more developed are: particularly hydroelectricity, the forest, but above all the mining sector, doped by the growth of the emerging countries and the reconstruction of public infrastructures.
The North contains " nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium, and also diamond ," according to Mr. Charest's enumeration.
"Twisted territory like France ," ," is our home, it's in Québec.
It's not just home to ours, it's in us ," he insisted, to show that he asked for a national founding myth.
Quebec was built a lot thanks to its relationship with the North, he stressed the rift towards gold in Abitibi, Quebequois engineering, James Bay.
"It belongs to us, it's our future," he added, having made a theatrical pause to contemplate the immense map behind him.
The North, 70 per cent of Quebec territory, comprises a portion of the Labrador that Quebec always claimed, the prime minister said during the press conference.
"It's a traditional position that all governments reiterated.
There's a boundary line in which they haven't agreed for a long time and, when the occasion is presented, I reiterate that position on the part of the government ," he said.
In addition, "With climate change, we have to rethink our vision of the North ," he said, speaking of the northwest passage, which will open every year due to ice smelting.
In this new geopolitical context, the Prime Minister believes that occupying the territory is a duty.
And the inhabitants of the territory, the First Nations and the Inuits?
The Prime Minister said the Québec government would be very attentive to them and that the project would benefit, particularly young people.
Two former ministers from two different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former MP of the Quebec Party of Ungava), have already started consultations about thirty communities.
An inter inter-ministerial committee chaired by Beno-t Pelletier and in which Julie Boulet (Transport and Natural Resources) has an important role to play.
The Prime Minister does not rule out an important meeting, "not necessarily a summit ," but a meeting at which "a common vision will be given ."
The North &apos; s development would proceed in accordance with the principles of sustainable development.
The Northern Plan "would Be Concerted, Orderly and Environmental-Virtient," he promised.
Jean-Charest described this "vision" as one that is in continuum with that of the Liberal prime ministers who preceded him.
"Like the liberals who preceded us, we've thought differently about transforming the challenges on new opportunities for growth and development.'
This was one of the recurring topics of the weekend, which began on Friday with a large cocktail to highlight the 50th anniversary of Jean Lesage's arrival in the power of the party.
One of the resolutions adopted proposes rebranding the Beauharnois Central in Embalse Adélard-Godbout.
" De Godbout, who created HydroQuebec, in Lesage that made the Revolution calm and the nationalization of hydroelectricity, in Bourassa that made James Bay our party knew how to forge the future," said Juan Charest.
