Indeed, the Prime Minister, who said in April that there would be no elections taking place in 2008 and certainly not in 2009, did not rule out the possibility of putting in place a vote this autumn, which the law allows.
He points out that his schedule is nevertheless full, particularly because of a mission of the federation council in China and a Quebec-New York York summit.
The weekend's general council supplied the Liberal boss with a platform containing nationalist resolutions, particularly "constitutional aspirations" that had no problem being accepted: the Quebec Liberal Party particularly calls for recognition of Québec's "fiscality" in the Constitution.
He would also like Quebec to appoint members of the Canadian Broadcasting and Telecommunications Council who would have the right to veto decisions taken in Québec.
