Free Opinion - Barrick Gold's Orwellian Speech
In its September 17 edition, Le Devoir published a letter from Patrick J. Garver, vice-president and director of the Canadian mining company Barrick Gold.
Following an Orwellian logic, Barrick Gold claims that the world's largest gold company attempted a six-million-euro libel trial against three authors and a small publishing house in order to provoke "transparent public debate."
If it is true that the judges are independent and unbiased, it is not the truth they judge, especially in the case of libel chases, but the law.
Unsurprisingly, this fundamental difference is completely obscured by Mr. Garver.
Why, according to Barrick Gold, the best way to conduct a public debate would harm anyone who dares to hold a position contrary to theirs?
"The outcome of the lawsuit is of little importance, the authors and the publisher of the book Noir Canada will have to spend considerable sums in order to prepare their defense and to fight, with very unequal weapons, against an army of lawyers supported by an almost unsubstantiated pocket of the mining company.
The tactic, in these unequal struggles, is often about spoiling the Defendants in order to impose an amicable settlement that will loosen the opposition in addition to scaring whoever will dare to re-examining the issue again.
Had Barrick Gold truly wanted a public debate, it is in front of the public that he would have had to present his vision of the facts.
Contrary to what is being done in several countries where Barrick Gold works, the debates in Canada are normally done by the change of ideas and not to stick behes.
It was therefore sufficient for the mining company to open its books, its files and establish a real debate, through the newspapers, the media, the publications and the financing of independent search teams that would have been able to clarify the issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn't seem to rely on public opinion and reject requests and bra letters for the Noir Canada authors.
Possibly Mr. Garver could you explain how you can ask for a "public" debate by despising its views?
According to the vice-president, it seems that this word designates only high-paid lawyers and not for all Canadians; that represents a very limited view of the concept.
Detractors also have the right to speak.
What you don't understand - or what you don't want to understand - Barrick Gold, is that the public that supports the authors and publishers of Noir Canada does it above all because it rejects the brutal form of mining.
This audience is willing to listen to Barrick and judging him, but on the basis of civilized changes of arguments, and not within the framework of what many people perceive as chasing-mordaza (persecuring the "public debate ," Barrick Gold has just placed a requirement regarding the authors of Noir Canada urging them not to use this word anymore).
In addition to Mr. Garver &apos; s ordeal speech, Barrick Gold's process is a direct attack on the freedom of university search and the truth, essential for all democratic societies.
It denies the right to cite credible fountains and to have a debate on facts and interpretations, which represent the basis of intellectual work.
If it is impossible to study and discuss subjects that displease the wealthy companies of this world in a country like Canada, under the grief of many persecutions, who will be able to do so?
If Barrick Gold truly wanted a public and transparent debate, it could do so in accordance with the scientific standards used by the authors of Noir Canada.
You've got a right to answer.
The university world, to which we belong, knows for a long time to manage debates and disagreements and resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold's disproportionate chase clearly shows that, contrary to what he claims, he doesn't look forward to a transparent debate.
It is a loss to the university world, but also to the public debate in Canada, and it's about time for governments to place devices that limit this kind of abusive chases.
Wall Street rescue operation - Democrats and Republicans agree
The Bush administration and congressional leaders, both Republicans and Democrats, finally reached an agreement yesterday on the most important economic rescue plan since the Great Depression of the 1930s.
The new version of the U.S. $700-billion-billion-dollar plan aimed at curbing the financial crisis shaking Wall Street for months has yet to be confirmed by both houses of Congress.
It foresees in particular: a better control on the part of those elected from its implementation, of new mechanisms that seek to ensure the reimbursement of the public money invested, the new provisions to contain the number of houses seized the non-payment, as well as a right of control over the wages paid to the leaders of the assisted companies.
U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Republican representative at the negotiating table, Senator Judd Gregg, announced the good news a few minutes after midnight yesterday, at the end of a marathon of negotiations lasted two days.
"We are making great progress towards an agreement that will be effective in the markets ," Bush administration representative Henry Paulson said.
"Everything has been done to cover the economics and ordinary Americans in the face of the current crisis on Wall Street," the Democrat explained.
"That's the fundamental point: if we don't do this, the traumatism, chaos and disruption to the daily lives of Americans will be immense," Republican Judd Gregg said.
Negotiations had ended very quickly in the last few hours because they feared the effect a further failure on investors' moody.
The last hour that had been fixed was the opening of the markets this morning.
The two nominees in the U.S. presidential elections, who have joined the negotiations late, were rather favorable for the agreement, without forgetting to squeeze their dissatisfaction with being forced to reach this limb.
Democratic senator Barack Obama confided certain provisions for reaching an agreement, in particular the one that would allow homeowners to retain their homes and the over-the-wage salaries of state-suspecting companies.
"When taxpayers are asked to take extraordinary measures because of the irre-responsibility of some, we must not be congratulated.
But these measures are necessary ," he said on American television.
his Republican opponent, John McCain, questioned on another network to find out if he would vote for the proposed plan, replied: "I'd like to see the details, but I'm looking forward to voting."
"The important points I read [show] that it's something we're going to have to digest and with which we will be able to advance.
He said the option of doing nothing is not acceptable.'
The $700-billion-billion plan aims to buy the dubious debts with taxpayers, the risk-to-risk and unvengetable assets of American financial institutions.
It comes after 14 months of a crisis caused by the eruption of a real estate bubble that first led to the sinking of risky mortgage loans before extending to other more common forms of credit securities (PCAAs) and provoking a liquidity freeze and disengagement from banks and financial institutions.
It's a group of Republican voters who had changed the first draft agreement last week.
We think we've emerged from stalemate since Democrats accepted that certain "toxic" assets of struggling companies are only insured by the government, rather than purchased.
With the aim of lining up roughness for the entire world in Congress, we also added a provision stipulating that if the government had to assume losses for five years with this program, it could impose new rates that would aim to help it recover money.
The new plan envisages the payment of a first part of $250 billion to which an additional $100 billion can be added if the president finds it necessary.
The payment of another $350-billion would have to be confirmed by another congressional vote.
In exchange for its help, Washington will get shares in the rescued companies that will be worth dividends in the event of a profit and that it will be able to re-sell when the storm will be past.
The federal government will have the possibility of delaying embargo procedures involving homes obtained with real estate loans under this scheme, as well as set limits on remuneration and cash compensation paid to the leaders of companies affected by the crisis.
The architects of the plan would like to see him put to the vote of the House of Representatives from today and to that of the Senate next Wednesday.
In spite of all his efforts, he is still unsure that his plan will be approved.
Some people continued to protest against this project yesterday, both on the part of the Republicans as well as the Democrats.
Republican Scott Garrett, a member of the chamber's finance committee, told his constituents that "with absolutely evidence, Washington doesn't want to understand."
"We have a very serious financial problem.
There's some solutions.
But it's not what it's about profiting from taxpayers.'
Democratic Rep. Brad Sherman, who's cheerleader of a small group in Congress, presents the problems differently, but comes to the same conclusion.
" Four hundred professors of the economy, among whom three Nobel laureates, wrote to tell us: we ask Congress not to rush and carefully examine the path that needs to be taken," he said yesterday.
At this very moment, in Europe, we once again had proof of the global nature of the crisis.
After an urgent meeting, the three Benelux countries announced the partial nationalization of the Fortis banking group for 11.2 billion euros ($16.3 billion).
Intervention by the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg had been necessary after the French bank BNP Paribas announced that it had relinquished its bankrupt competitor since losing 2 billion euros ($2.9-billion dollars) during the U.S. risky real estate loan crisis.
Fortis employs 85,000 people and is one of the 20 largest European banks and the ten largest insurers.
"This isn't lost money," Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos said yesterday.
On the other hand, we regain a right to vote at the bank and influence.
It's just something savers and families appreciated now that times are uncertain.'
During this time, on the other side of the groove, the British government seemed to want to nationalize another bank suffering from the financial crisis.
According to the English press, the government would take care of all the outstanding loans at Bradford and Bringley bank and more than 41 billion euros ($75.5 billion) of real estate loans.
The Spanish bank Santander announced yesterday that it is the one who would purchase the branches and bank accounts of the British bank.
The Bradford bank and Bringley would be the second bank, after Northern Rock, that the British government would have to nationalise from the beginning of this financial crisis.
