What'd they produce at the Zuschlag &amp; Co bill factory?
Very soon, on October 8, the criminal proceedings will be initiated against János Zuschlag and his accomplices.
The politician and his accomplices are accused of committing fraud of significant damage, in a business manner, continuously and in an organized manner in addition to other crimes, including the falsification of documents.
According to the Public Prosecutor &apos; s Office they used the funds obtained in the public competitions for their own purposes, on many occasions justifying the fulfillment of the tasks with false invoices.
The public funds and that of the municipalities - in its entirety about 65 million HUF - stopped in part in the treasury of the Fiatal Baloldal organization and in part in the pocket of private individuals.
The method, released by the investigators as well, was to justify the collection of the funds obtained in the public competitions with false invoices behind which there was no real execution, the various associations never fulfilled their obligations assumed.
According to the accusation, most of the invoices were fake.
To do this they obtained the heels in the market of the Józsefváros District and the circular seals were manufactured in the city of Kiskunhalas.
And the question that in the name of which company the counterfeit invoice should be carried out was decided among others on that tour of the Superior Region of the Balathon where János Zuschlag sent István Lados.
The latter confessed that for this purpose he had to choose hostels and restaurants.
He requested an invoice for his consumption and these served as a model to draw up the invoices they presented, among others in the competitions of the Ministry of Youth and Sports Affairs, at the Ministry of Social and Labor Affairs, as well as in the City of the Capital.
These invoices were mostly signed by István Lados and his accomplice András Eri, who according to our information rather wrote the reports necessary to obtain public subsidies.
During one of his "study trips" Sidos he chose a brewery.
According to two invoices issued in 2003 by Zuschlag and his accomplices, this gastronomic center provided the food for a group of ninety-something people from the Biztos Pont Egyes-let Association.
However - according to the centre's manager - so many people don't fit at the same time.
Likewise, the invoice issued in the summer of 2002 for the Jobb Jévaért Foundation - later called Egy-tt a Harmadik Evezredérért - was full of formal and contextual errors.
Because a hostel in the town of Badacsonytomaj had only three bedrooms and more than ten people couldn't stay there in one night, however the bill consisted of a hundred and thirty people.
"By fantasy invoice manufacturers also exaggerated when they pointed to the gastronomic center Pigal Kft. Because in the holding of books it - according to the information given to the Public Prosecutor's Office - there were no documents in the name of Jobb J?vaért and Hoppá.
The company &apos; shooter, the company's manager, informed hvg.hu that when he was summoned by the prosecutor &apos; s office and showed him the invoices, he already knew that these were false documents.
"On the seal the name of our company and the NIF appeared in the wrong place."
He added that in addition to the formal problems it did not agree that according to the bill a hundred people were housed, since they have only eleven bedrooms in which so many people don't fit.
In addition, the bill also included meals, while the restaurant hasn't worked for a long time.
Enthusiastic invoice manufacturers were also not informed that the travel agency chosen in Budapest exclusively organizes trips abroad, so in the cash payment bill they foolishly indicated as service a trip within Hungary, which also did not coincide with the agency's practice of issuing invoices.
In addition - as was known during the investigation - they also included VAT in the document, even though travel outside the territory of the European Union was exempt from taxes.
Among the other other things the VAT measure was also incorrect in the four documents issued by the Hotel Rév-l?p Kft. for the Jobb J?vért foundation, since the services of that company are not loaded with a 25 but only with 15 per cent VAT, in addition the hotel's employees only extended machine-made invoices however the invoice was manual.
In addition the "expeditors" made other mistakes as well, as in the case of a company in which they placed in the invoice as an activity carried out the road transport to which - it was known during the investigations of the prosecutor's office - it does not carry out the company founded as an investment consultant.
Equally because of formal differences - inappropriate format of the invoice, fake seal - it has been known that the invoices issued for Egy-tt to Harmadik "vezredérért Alapítvány, for XXI" Századi Kulturális Egyes-let and for Biztos Pont.
As in Ravenna Bt.'s case or KunfeHértó's Deportivo Camp, whose representatives denied issuing those invoices.
The forgeers also did not pay attention to the fact that when the Fiatal Városért Egyes-let group eating the HIB Kft of Nyíregyháza was in the process of winding up was in the process of liquidation, that is why the person in charge of the liquidation process - according to our information - denied having issued such a document.
Consciously expressed the invoices.
Another group of fake invoice manufacturers are made up of those who were aware that they should issue fictitious invoices.
Fundación Esély Budapestért Alapítvány , at the request of Fiatal Baloldal - Ifjú Szocialisták, issued an invoice worth about four million HUF on behalf of the Fundación Egyátt a Harmadik "vezredért."
According to the accusation, one of the defendant Ludovic Gy?rfi was persuaded to issue fictitious invoices, Ludovic Gy?rfi to issue fictitious invoices.
In the same way he collaborated with Zuschlag and his complicit the Biogate Kft., currently under the liquidation process, which in 2003 supplied invoices worth approximately five million HUF to the Jobb J.v.érért Alapítvány foundation , the association Atlasz Egyes-let and to the association V.rsfeny-Egyes-let , without real economic activity behind it.
There was also no movement of capital either, because they then cancelled the prepared invoices but they forgot to record this entity in their documents.
The next group of fake invoicing manufacturers includes those who have some ties to the defendants.
For example, Ivan Kubatov was the surrogacy for the Ukrainian manager of the fictional-based Delta Trio SL based in Budapest, which dealt with the trade in fuels and vehicles.
This company issued numerous invoices worth approximately HUF 18 million in the months of October, November and December 2005 on the sale of computer means.
According to the data of the investigation these invoices were purchased by Kiskunhalas Cash Investment Kft, which has been under liquidation since September 14, 2005.
The manager of this company was a Romanian to whom Iván Kubatov should also have released the documents related to the company between 2002 and 2005.
We have information that Kubatov acknowledged during the investigation that he issued on behalf of the company invoices for the Atlasz, for the Hoppá and for Egy-tt to Harmadik "vezredérérért Alapítgéy collecting the amount himself."
The Fa-Bani Jos Faipari Kft and Jos-Investment Bt. were fused by Romanian bricklayers at the express request of Ivan Kubatov.
However, none of the companies actually functioned because - according to the defendant - that they did not receive a job.
But he took advantage of these companies, since at the request of his accomplice the lawyer Zsolt Szente, who prepared the registration documents of the company, issued invoices on behalf of the companies for the organizations linked to Zuschlag: for the Atlasz and Y-Generáció.
Kubatov on behalf of Cash Investment, Delta Trió, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade gave a total of 15 invoices to different entities worth HUF 14.2 million.
The Public Prosecutor &apos; s Office considered that Kubatov received the VAT and 10 per cent of the amount of the invoices.
That man even took advantage of the Szi-Beri-Comandite society to issue fictitious invoices used in the Biztos Pont competition.
In January 2006 they presented Táncsics Mihály Alpaítvány (TMA), a public utility entity of the Socialist Party, a public utility entity of the Socialist Party, a competition in January 2006.
From the contest they obtained 500,000 HUF respectively, but they did not use the money for the objectives of the competition.
For this not to be undisciplined, five fictitious invoices received from the Works Team Kft submitted to the TMA.
These were the invoices found during the search of the dispatch that Árpád Kemecsei had at the headquarters of the Ministry of Education.
Some of the defendants will probably recount in the trial - as they did in the Prosecutor &apos; s Office - the course of falsification of the invoices.
The most interesting thing may be the confession of the main defendant János Zuschlag himself, if he decides to dismantle everything.
Because their confessions so far have not been very detailed and although he repeatedly vowed to talk about everything, this hasn't done so to the present.
The question is: what interest does he have to do so?, because perhaps in exchange for the return of most of the money - fifty million HUF - the socialists came to an agreement with him so that Zusi assumes all responsibilities.
On the other hand, for these days he could even be offended, since in order to alleviate his prison conditions he requested house arrest, but he was repeatedly denied.
While the many perpetrators of much-major economic crimes, who have never retrieveed the money, stroll freely or can at least wait in their own home for sentencing, he doesn't have a chance for that.
Psychology: Why do we spend more money?
Auction participants are likely to spend more money and overestimate the product.
Neurologists and an economists at New York University discovered the neurological - economic foundations for this well-known and apparently irrational behavior.
According to the study published in the journal Science, people don't want to lose in competitions such as eBay auctions, so they prefer to pay even an excessive price.
Scientists tried to establish the neurological scheme of this "game" through experimental auctions.
One group of volunteers played lottery, the other was participating in an auction and in the meantime was being examined through the functional magnetic resonance resonance imaging system (fMRI), the active areas of the brain of people participating in the experiment.
In both games the participants could make money, but during the auctions, where it is a fundamental element to bet more than the other, losing the gambling the operation of the complex called stratum located beneath the cerebral crust intensified.
This area of the brain, in addition to so many other functions, plays a role in the "self-principle" mechanism of the nervous system.
According to scientists, the increase in gambling is explained as a prevention so as not to provoke an intense stratum reaction.
To substantiate his theory, the economists of the scientific group also carried out a financial follow-up study.
In this experiment three groups bet against a person under different circumstances.
The control group was simply informed of the real value of the product in question, and its members gambled.
Another group was also promised that if they won, they would receive an extra $15 prize.
On the other hand, the third group received exactly $15 before the auction, but in the event of losing the auction the members of the group even lost that money.
Thus the only difference between the two groups was that for one the triumph was highlighted and to the other the failure.
However, by verifying the hypothesis of the neurologists, members of the group who played "against failure" were willing to bet even more than the members of the control group and the "winner group."
"These results cannot be deciphered from any previously existing economic theory.
For those until now explained the phenomena of the auctions only with the joy of winning and with the prevention of the risks.
Visual neurological methods alone helped resolve this contradiction between the two theories, so we now come up with a third explanation," said Andrew Schotter, a professor at New York University's faculty of economics.
Hepatitis
Around five hundred million people worldwide suffer from chronic hepatitis B, and in Hungary chronic liver disease is seen as one of the main causes of death.
It is estimated at one hundred thousand the number of infected people in this country, but only seven thousand receive treatment because many of the sick do not know that they are infected.
Hepatitis is the common name for different liver diseases caused by the virus.
Symptoms resemble a lot a lot; liver and jaundice infection are characteristic, but their pathogenic agents and the diseases caused are very different.
Hepatitis viruses dependent on the pathogen are propagated through two main forms: hepatitis A &amphepatitis A &amp; e.g. (e.g. with water or infected food), hepatitis B, C, D, and other viruses with bodily mood (hope, vagina mood, saliva).
It is advisable for travelers to protect themselves through vaccines against viruses A and B, there are no Vaccinations without vaccines.
Symptoms of Hepatitis A occur two or three weeks after consuming the infected food is manifested through general poor condition, weakness, abdominal pain and nausea.
After that the jaundice appears on the skin and white of the eager, the excrement becomes clear, urinating of dark color and healing can last even two - three weeks.
Annually one million people die in the world in cancer and liver cirrhosis caused by hepatitis B, the most serious forms of this disease.
The main reason for this is that according to the calculations, only one in ten infected people receives pharmacological treatment.
Hepatitis C can also have very serious consequences: without treatment it can cause liver cirrhosis and even cancer.
Most infected people don't know he's sick because this pathological agent gradually destroys the liver's cells and often the first symptoms only appear 20-30 years later.
Premature recognition, systematic medical control, is therefore very important.
It is much more common in three groups: people who had blood transfusions before 1991, drug users and those who wear tattoos.
Currently there are about 200 million people in the world suffering from chronic Hepatitis.
As this disease can be discovered with a routine lab control as well, it's worth consulting with the family's clerk when you experience an unknown symptom.
The disease is cured if it is discovered in time, it is possible to exterminate the virus of the organism, so contagion can be suppressed and cancer prevented.
September 30 is Hepatitis World Day, which was first organized in 2004 by organizations representing the sick.
Its objective is to draw attention to the causes of chronic liver infections caused by viruses and the importance of their premature recognition.
