According to the result of a recent investigation, religious faith has a soothing effect relieving the death of martyrs of Catholicism.
"Scientists at the University of Oxford carried out an interesting experiment: they analyzed that the religious belief in how it influences human grief - the British newspaper Telegraph reported.
Within the framework of this unique experiment they applied an electric clash to 12 Catholic believers and to the same number of atheists, meanwhile everyone was watching a painting of the Virgin Mary.
According to the results, Catholic believers were able to exclude most of their grief.
Thanks to swimming technique of the human brain, it could be verified that Catholic believers were able to activate that part of their brain that is responsible for the regulation of pain.
This experiment is the first in the series of studies in which, alongside academics, scientists, philosophers and theologians from the different departments of the University also participate.
The analyzing occurred as follows; they fixed a sparkling instrument to the patient's left hand, which applied the electric shock.
Investigators asked the participants of the experiment to fix their gaze on two paintings; the Virgen Mary, the 17th-century Sassoferrato work and the La Lady of Leonardo da Vinci of the 15th-century.
Investigators had the hope that the spectacle of Our Lady Mary would provoke a certain religious mood among the faithful, while they chose Leonardo's oil because it wasn't too different from each other, and they thought it had a reassuring effect.
The patients spent half an MRI scanner in that period receiving 20 electric crashes in four successive parts while watching the religious-themed painting or the other.
The Catholics stated that by looking at the Virgin Mary they felt in "safety" like "protected people," "they calmed down and peace reigned in their hearts."
Observing the picture of religious themes they felt 12 per cent less sorrow than when they looked at Leonardo's work.
The psychologist Miguel Farias, who also participated in the experiment, thinks that with any very shocking image you can achieve a similar effect with the atheists as well, you only have to find the right image.
"We should find the portrait of a person for whom they feel very positively, such as by a mother or a parent," the psychologist said.
Anglican Bishop Tom Wright celebrated the outcome of the investigation.
"The exercise of the faith should and in many cases it really changes our personality," Wright added.
After these formidable results there is only one question: what effect would it have on the regulation of sorrrow if the Madonna of Leonardo or another painter of his stature were shown to the faithful?
Was the vision of a high-quality Madonna Lady to yield even better results?
It's what you're saying about yourself.
On its 10th birthday the Google can say: it can be found both in people's pockets, and in the sky above his head; he knows where they spent his vacation, what doctors they visit and what they are interested in.
But according to Eric Schmidt, one of the company's executives, this isn't enough yet.
On the other, skeptics who make a parallel with the Orwelian vision don't see the essence of Web 2.0.
Eric Schmidt recently told the Financial Times that Google's main objective is to collect as much personal data as possible about its users, and this is necessary in order to be able to meet the original objectives of Google's founders: "Let's bring together all the information in the world into a system."
According to the leader of Google, the company even after ten years of work that swung to the World is still in the initial phase of the effort to be able to deal with all the information.
To the question what is going to happen in five years' time Schmidt replied: "I would like the Google to be able to answer the questions such as "what to do tomorrow?" or "what would be the job that would best suit me?" - however, the software will not be able to answer questions that seem so simple until there is enough user information.
Not all netizens are aware that during their browsing on the Web the search-wrought keywords, the IP addresses and the pages where they passed at the end of the searching are stored.
Thanks, for example, Personalized Search that debuted two years ago, users can store their own browsing history, they can re-consute what they were looking for before, what they opened and thanks to this history it will generate much more personalized searches - in this same logic will be based on which the system will offer us products and services based on the preferences previously defined and stored.
"What will happen if they take advantage of my data against myself, if they collect information about my activities?" ask those users who worry about seeing Google's information monopoly.
In most cases the potential represented by the company is what can be fearful and not remorse, fear for punishment, longing for freedom or rebellion against power.
However, it is an already confirmed fact that not only do they preserve, but in justifiable cases they also give to third parties all the expressions sought, in these cases there is nothing to protect the personal rights of netizens.
The most debated example in recent times was the coordinated action against Brazilian paedophiles, when Google gave the Brazilian government the information of its website, called Orkut, to identify paedophiles.
On the other hand, netizens who cared about the rights of the individuals in May were alleviated when the company together with the EFF, an organization for the defense of electronic rights, successfully demonstrated before the court that it was right in its dispute with the FBI: according to the sentence, Google will not be able to be obligated, through none of the points of the constitution to provide any information requested in secret by the FBI.
(At the same time, the federal government managed on the pretext of tightening the child protection law adopted years ago, about fifty thousand Web addresses and about a thousand phrases searched, but without obtaining the search lists of individual users, nor the complete lists of web pages and keywords of the last two months.)
That's quite conflicting with the opinion on Google: it is criticised by those afraid to supply their personal data because they think it damages their intimacy, their private lives by starting to use Google's services, and they rebelliously against multinational conservative image companies.
In the meantime, American geek culture and its enthusiastic followers, netizens and users of other countries, see in them the future generation, the "avant-garde corporation," thus not to its rival, Microsoft, catalogued by its Windows as conqueror of the world, as much as they both fight for the same objective.
This can happen because Google, contrary to Microsoft, does not ask users for any identification code or money for their apps, the code is open and the free program package is constantly updated and seems more youthful, more collaborating than the practice of the official software giant.
But it may also be because geeks see precisely the very problematic relationship between people's rights and the Internet that occurred precisely by one of the keywords most mentioned in recent years: Web 2.0.
The Web 2.0 is about collaboration, leaving intentional footprints behind us and taking advantage of the available communication channels, and while the measurement of these varies individually, the essence remains the same: to show ourselves.
A normal young man can have a profile on numerous collective pages, from Facebook to Xanga, and can even link them with other services, such as Twitter or the last.fm that offers a list of their music habits, then they can be included in a tracking system such as the FriendFeed or the Blip.fm, you can also have a blog, in which the most recent photo galleries that are stored on Flickr or Picasa automatically appear.
We can know numerous cases from which it emerges that for example the girlfriend, when she sees the flow of photographs the next morning of the common friends, finds out who her fraudulent boyfriend spent the previous night or they can lay off the employee of a company because he writes his sincere opinion on his blog about the management of the company: one thing is certain, the definition of private life was completely revalued.
A person's "on-line" life is what she displays of herself - this is the definition that hides behind today's youth behavior.
And whoever wants to hide something, or at least aims to dodge Google's monopoly, he can do it (starting for example with the Scroogle that uses Google's search system, but leaves no footprints).
There are many people who don't want to register on the collective pages to prevent them from being able to find them, but you can replace "on-line" apps, from text-writing software to image and news-reading applications with alternative versions that don't connect to gigantic servers or prepare the most perfect profile possible about us.
Public opinion almost openly waits the day Google shows its true face.
The company at its current value of $156 billion is worth more than IBM ($140 billion), Intel ($124bn) or Coca-Cola ($110 billion), AdWords/AdSense advertising surfaces threaten the revenue of advertising agencies, while services that are increasingly introduced cause serious headaches not only for Microsoft but also numerous other software developers.
Criticized observers, however, will also find alarming contradictions: for example the senior management categorically rejected the employee's protest after doubling the price of the company's nursery nursery, considered the unlimited food number of employees too expensive, but all of this is nothing compared to the fact that in 2006 the Google ceded in the face of pressure from the Chinese government and the company according to the agreement leaked any information that violated the good morality and interests of the Chinese regime.
One of the company's internal regulations says they won't announce any new services in advance, but only when it's already ready to work, what's certain is that the foreseeable problems of the next few months and years will be able to revolve into the world: while some believe that Google's scanning and book-and-file project will acquire such dimensions that the company will soon be cited by the U.S. Supreme Court, others are afraid of its Health system, because in it we can create our own full health profile, with previous analyses, with our doctors and our problems.
And the fact that Google is going to create a genetic database will be able to provide a good raw material for sci-fi writers, the Scroogled, a novel by the popular Cory Doctorow, describes Orwelian form that future, in which a man only of Google's data can describe himself alarmingly well.
Two minors helped erase the perpetrator of the two-studded murder of the city of Ezd.
The police interrogated two minors about the double murder of Ezd who allegedly helped erase the footsteps of the crime - the spokeswoman for the General Commissariat of Public Order in Borsod province said on Tuesday.
The young women recognized the crime that was charged to them, and are currently accused of perpetrators of the crime.
The two minors - according to information from the Hungarian news agency these are two girls - will be able to defend themselves at large.
For the complicity they can receive up to 5 years in prison.
The accused of the double murder of Ezd - a 23-year-old man - has been in pre-trial detention since Sunday.
He is charged with the crime of murdering several people with premeditation and greed.
The 23-year-old was revealed to o'clock a large sum to a 53-year-old.
The defendant did not want to repay his debt, so on Wednesday last week he summoned his creditor to the headquarters of an Ezd company, where he killed him and then also killed the 9-year-old daughter of the victim.
According to information from the MTI, the young man prepared much earlier to perpetrate the crime, as he committed the murder with a weapon previously hidden at the crime scene.
With an expert an expert, the weapon and the circumstances of its use.
The Warsaw Stock Exchange submitted an offer to buy the Prague Stock Exchange
The Warsaw Stock Exchange offered two hundred million euros - about five billion Czech kronor - for one hundred per cent of the shares of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, president of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, confirmed the offer made by the Poles, even though the Czechs have previously excluded the Warsaw Stock Exchange from the potential new owners of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The reason is that the Warsaw Stock Exchange is in the hands of the state.
Ninety-nine per cent of the shares are in the hands of the U.S. state which in a few months' time wants to sell 60 per cent.
The Czech economic diary E15 reported that Poles &apos; offer has been the tallest among those interested in the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to the Czech press, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlantic Stock Exchange, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and the Deutsche B?rse have so far offered.
Petr Koblic, the boss of the Prague Stock Exchange, stated last week that among those interested the worst new owner would be the Warsaw Stock Exchange and did not rule out the possibility that with it, the Prague Stock Exchange would disappear in the future.
Analysts estimate the value of the Prague Stock Exchange at around 5 to 10 billion kronor.
According to the shareholder agreement, approximately 90 per cent of the shares could be sold.
The majority owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is the investment bank Patria Finance with a stake of approximately 25 per cent, followed by Ceská Sporitelna Bank with $14.7 and Tiger Holding Four S. a.r.l with 13.5 per cent.
On the financial crisis from the point of view of the European press
Leading European diaries in their comments on Tuesday devoted to the deepening international financial crisis speak of a "second of 9 September."
The global financial catastrophe is due to the American president - he believes The Republic of Rome - the president hasn't even been able to get the support of his own party's hastily de facto bill, whose aim is to solve this desperate situation...
Instead of a new "Pearl Habor," spoken of by billionaire investor Warren Buffett, it's more correct to talk about a "second 9/11."
According to a part of the Madrid press, Washington is the scapegoat.
According to El Mundo, the mistake of the political leadership of the U.S. was the one that brought the world economy to the brink of the abyss.
There is a growing danger of the collapse of the American monetary system and this will then have an impact on the whole world.
Although Bush's plan wasn't perfect, after the congressional vote arises the danger that Washington does not offer a political instrument to deal with the crisis.
The world's economy is suspended in the air - precisely when one of the greatest transformations in the history of capitalism takes place.
"You can't ignore this threat," says the Tages-Anzeiger in Geneva.
Some states today may still throw life-threatening beets at wobbly financial institutions, thus being able to save them from bankrupt with taxpayers" money.
"But if as the domino crumbles even more bank, then the new funds will no longer be enough or the new improvisations.
In the long-running, it is necessary for the Member States of the European Union to put aside their nationalist selfishness and centralize control over fragmented financial markets.
Crisis financial institutions received more than EUR 100 billion on Monday alone - the Viennese Der Standard recalls, adding that worries are becoming more and more real that, as the Americans did, European taxpayers will also have to pay plenty of money to solve the crisis.
So far, the EU has not been seriously concerned with the crisis that passes as a blive through the Old Continent .
The "savers" will not immediately have to submit hundreds of millions of euros, but it would be nice if they defined: in what cases is it justified to use taxpayers' funds in order to avoid a greater disease?
Smart car mimics bogey flight
What would be the best method to develop technologies in order to avoid collisions, if not the imitation of existing barrier sensors in nature?
For example the abhorros.
In a stunning project presented at CEATEC in Japan, bumblebees provided the starting point for Nissan engineers to design the next generation of colluding prevention systems.
The Nissan, in cooperation with the University of Tokyo, designed the BR23C automotive robotic steering system imitator of nature, to which it was given the characteristics of bumblebees, with the ultimate aim of creating a system that can dodge all colliding.
During the flight each bubecule creates its own particular oval space that actually looks a lot like our safety protective capsule - Kazuhhiro Doi, technological director of RP and of development and research, explained in the exhibition Kazuhhiro Doi, technological director of RP and of development and research.
They play an important role the composite eyes of bubbles who are able to see in all directions at an angle of more than 300 degrees.
To copie the function of the composite eyes, engineers used the laser-beard measurement system.
It identifies obstacles in front of the BR23C within a distance of 2 meters, at an angle of 180 degrees, calculates its distance and sends a signal to the microprocessor of the vehicle that is immediately transformed into a maneuver to prevent the collision.
In a fraction of seconds the hurdle is identified, then the robot of the vehicle imitating the movements of bumblebees, modifies the direction by rotating the wheels as necessary.
Contrary to any system currently existing, this maneuver is totally instinctive.
"If it weren't, the vehicle robot couldn't react quickly enough to dodge the hurdles," explains Toshiyuki Andoh, head of the Mobility Lab and the vehicle robot project.
It must react immediately and instinctively, since this technology corresponds to the most vulnerable inner layer of the safety protective capsule, in a layer within which all types of collisions are currently considered inevitable.
The whole process must reflect what a bubble is doing to dodge other bumblebees.
This process must take place in the blink of an eye.
Unlike bumblebees, however, the movement can't go up, down or diagonal, but it can only be two-dimensional and exclusively in the direction of spin of the wheels.
In this way, instead of the infinite possibilities that exist for bumblebees to dodge other bumblebees, here they only used rotary movement as a possibility for the robot of the car rotary movement and the acceleration also the braking.
The BR23C uses lithium-polymer batteries that are lighter and their energy density is higher, which means they have a longer lifespan-suspecting duration than lithium-ion.
According to Andoh, what is important is the instinctive and intelligent reaction and not the processing capacity of the data, let alone that of storage.
This appliance in every second must only process a few entry signals and react as appropriate.
You don't need to store this data or react with an instinctive logic based on the data from previous maneuvers, thus using only a few dozen kilobytes of memory.
Memories of gigabytes or megabytes are not needed.
This is the first step in developing the technology to immediately dodge the hurdles, which according to Nissan's calculations, in ten years' time it will be able to be incorporated into serial-made models.
Another possibility for its application is to incorporate the unit into the GPS satellite browsing system to bring the driver to its destination, dodging existing obstacles on its path.
