The written version of the $700-billion financial bailout package has been prepared and will be implemented in several phases in order to buy the bad loans from the banks.
Half the money will be spent in the first phase.
According to East Coast hours during further consultations that lasted until after midnight on Saturday, congressional leaders reached an agreement around the $700 billion financial bailout package in total, after several days of discussion, Reuters reported.
"We've made significant progress," Nancy Pelosi, the Democratic chairwoman of the U.S. congresswoman, said it would only be formalized by the agreement in a document.
Pledging on the package was supported by both candidates for the presidency, Barack Obama and John McCain.
Henry Paulson, the Minister of Finance, speaking about the outcome of the commitment, said: "This program will work and be efficient but further measures are needed. According to Pelosi, it's not about saving Wall Street, it's all about turning the cart of the economy.
The written version of the package was published prior to the opening of the Asian markets, i.e. Sunday before 8 o'clock in the evening, according to Washington hours (i.e. before two o'clock in the early morning of Monday, according to Central European hours), without causing a spectacular jump in the stock exchanges.
The 2008 economic stabilization emergency bill would vote on the emergency bill for economic stabili in the first Monday the U.S. Congress and then the U.S. Senate.
U.S. President George W. Bush on Saturday night consulted with Pelosi and then the White House also celebrated news about the deal.
"We are pleased with the progress made during the night and highly appreciating the efforts of both parties to stabilize our financial markets and protect our economy," White House spokesman Tony Fratto said.
The program differs from the Bush administration's original proposal to spend all of $700 billion to buy poor-cipronged credit fund securities.
Now you can only spend half of the $700 billion, using $250-billion immediately after congress is likely to vote, on Monday, the Ministry of Finance will buy low-rated credit securities and the fate of another $100 billion depends on the president's decision.
This program will be controlled by a special agency, in which the president of the Federal Reserve will also participate.
Half of the $700-billion could be spent after a new congressional decision.
According to the law the Ministry of Finance can apply a combined tactic: it can simultaneously purchase bad loans, mortgages and securities of credit, as well as other "poisonous" type loans as well.
Purchase can be made at auctions either directly - competent persons of the ministry reported.
The law allows the affected companies to participate in an insurance program where the finance portfolio guarantees the troubling elements, for which the companies will pay the amount corresponding to the foreseeable damages.
Henry Paulson stated: "The law thus ensures the necessary financial means, that they can even reach 700 billion dollars to meet the urgent needs of the American financial system, whether it is the purchase of bad financial means, its insurance or the prevention of risks that could lead to the collapse of some large financial institutions.
"We do everything we need to be ready to introduce the law as soon as they sign it," he added.
On the other hand, the bosses of the banks that are in distress and are waiting for the state's help will be able to count on the official limitation of their compensation, which the American slang simply calls golden parachutes.
Only in some specific cases will payments be limited, in addition the limitations depend on the form of assistance of the finance ministry.
The limitation means that if the ministry purchases as part of an auction, an extra tax will be imposed on the dinner parachutes, unless the bank's failed boss retires.
Upon buying the bad credits directly, the government will intervene aggressively to ensure bosses" compensation is not exaggerated.
In addition to the above, there will not be a change in existing gold parachute contracts, the limitations relate only to the new contracts to stimulate participation in the programme as well.
"This is the first case in U.S. history that a legal limitation will be in place for the income of the CEOs," said the measure Democrat Barney Frank, chairman of the committee of Congress, responsible for the financial services.
The State will also have the right to acquire shares of the banks participating in the program, thus reducing the risk of taxpayers, since they will ultimately be able to even make a profit from the transaction as soon as the economy recovers and with it the bank.
According to the bill over the next five years the president will have to submit to Congress a proposition on how to return the losses of the financial sector to taxpayers.
Of course on both sides there were those who expressed their reservations about the project.
"That's not what U.S. citizens entrusted, that is to say they're not protecting taxpayer dollars," said California Republican congressman Darrell Issa.
His Ohio Democratic colleague Mary Kaptur said the measures were urged by those responsible for the crumbling of Wall Street.
Industrial prices continue to rise
As of August 2008, domestic industrial sales prices increased by 0.1 per cent compared to last month &apos; stealth and 12.9 per per cent compared to the prices of August 2007, the Central Bureau of Statistics (KSH reported).
Industrial export prices rose by 1.1 per cent from July 2008 and fell 3.9 per per cent over the previous year.
And as a result of changes in domestic and export prices, industrial production prices increased by 0.7 per cent over the previous month and by 3.2 per cent compared to the previous year.
Industrial sales prices rose by just one minimum, at 0.1 per cent.
Domestic sales prices in the processing industry sectors rose minimally, at 0.1 per cent compared to July.
The largest increase in the magnitude of prices occurred in August 2008 in the leather and footwear production sector (2.8 per cent), mainly due to an increase of 4.6 per cent per month in bags and belts and an increase of 2.5 per cent of footwear by 2.5 per cent.
The higher level of the price of plastic and chemical base materials resulted in a significant monthly increase in the prices of the chemical production branch (1.6 per per cent).
There was a price decrease in the petrochemical sector (1.4 per cent), in the production of electrical machines and instruments (0.9 per cent), as well as - to a minimum extent - in the timber processing sectors (0.1 per per cent).
They didn't change the prices of paper manufacturing, publishers and printers.
In the rest sectors of the processing industry there was an increase in prices between 0.2 per cent and 0.9 per cent.
In the supply of electricity, gas, heat and water that represents a significant weight within domestic trade, an increase of 0.3 per per cent was recorded in the month of August, mainly as a result of the effect of the increase in official prices from July 1.
In a 12-month comparison - in domestic sales of the processing industry - the largest price increase was recorded in the petrochemical sector (32.4 per cent).
There was an increase of 13.2 per per cent in the metal processing sector, while in the food, beverage-and-small-to-boggling industry there was an increase of 11 per cent.
There was a decrease in prices in the textile industry (2.5 per cent), as well as the manufacture of electrical machines and instruments (0.3 per per cent).
In the rest of the processor sectors the pace of price growth was between 0.6 percent and 9.1 per cent.
Over the past year in the processing industry globally, prices rose by 10.2 per cent.
The prices of the electricity, the supply of gas, heat and water surpassed the prices of the previous year by 18.7 per cent.
In the month of August, the largest monthly increase in prices ( 0.7 per per cent) was in the investment goods sectors among the different sectors of domestic trade.
There was no change from the previous month, prices increased with 0.3 per per cent in the prices of the energy sector and the intermediate sectors, there was no change from the previous month.
In August 2008, compared to the same month of the previous year, the largest increase in prices occurred in the energy and intermediate industries (16.8 per cent).
In the production branches of consumer goods there was a decrease of 6.8 per cent and investment goods by 1.4 per cent.
The export prices of the HUF-fematched industry were up 1.1 per cent from the previous month, and compared with 12 months, there was a 3.9 per cent decline.
The quotation of our national currency in a month was reduced with 1.7 per cent against the euro and by 7.1 per cent against the dollar, compared to August 2007, strengthened with 7.5 per cent against the euro and 15.9 per cent against the dollar.
Next year you won't need visas to go to the United States anymore?
He believes there's plenty of possibilities for the U.S.-Hungarian agreement to enter into force around January 12 on visa waiver - the foreign affairs minister talked about it in Chicago, to the Hungarian residents there.
In his opinion "practically everything depends on the electronic access and control system being built on time on the other side of the Atlantic."
At the same time, he warned that in the new system the entrance grant will disappear, but tourists before leaving the country of origin will have to register.
From now on, the head of Hungarian diplomacy is holding talks in Washington.
He will meet with Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice handing the Median Cross with the Star of the Order of Merit of the Republic of Hungary and hold negotiations with the international policy advisors of the presidential nominees, Democrat Barack Obama and Republican John McCain.
Both Barack Obama and McCain are in favor of the banks' financial bailout package.
Barack Obama and John McCain, both presidential nominees of the two major U.S. parties on Sunday secured their support for the administration &apos; s $US700-brong banking aid program, on the other hand, there are politicians demanding the resignation of the Finance Minister.
The representatives of the Congress and the administration, after several marathon-type negotiations, in the early hours of Sunday announced that an agreement was reached of principles on the financial rescue package of the banks.
The financial package aimed at resolving the Wall Street crisis and defrosting the frozen credit market became the central theme of the presidential election campaign.
Both Obama and McCain want to show that they are more able than their rival to get the country out of the economic crisis.
According to the most recent polls, when economic issues came to the fore, Obama regained a certain advantage and McCain &apos; s advantage he had obtained after the Republicans" convention with Sarah Palin's candidacy for vice presidency disappeared.
At a Detroit campaign rally last Sunday, Obama expressed his hope that Congress will quickly pass the $700 billion package bill.
He pointed out that the proposal includes consumer protection points, which he himself supported.
"We have to act now and responsibly (...) the employment, the savings of you are in jeopardy and the stability of the entire economy," he said before a crowd of 35,000 people.
He didn't miss the opportunity to describe McCain's economic concepts as obsolete.
In an interview with the ABC McCain explained that "we will all barely swallow this (...) but doing nothing is simply not a viable path."
The Republican senator said that the proposal includes the establishment of a control committee to the minister of fineness and the limitation of the compensation of the executives, he was the one who insisted on that.
Obama told CBS that McCain didn't play a major role in the queries, instead he consulted daily with the minister.
Congresswoman Nancy Pelosi said on Sunday that congressmen are likely to vote as early as Monday on the bill.
Press review of the German-speaking area
"The right-wing caucus is untouched, only that within the group the most conscious voters became," Der Spiegel says of the CSU's weak election result in Bavaria.
Haider and his followers are poised to rule in Austria to prevent a red-haired coalition.
Der Spiegel (spiegel.de)
In the e-edition of the left-leaning German magazine Franz Walter in his article entitled "The Bavarian Revolution devours his parents," he analyzes the reasons for the dramatic defeat of the CSU.
According to the renowned political scientist, CSU Eymergence had begun long before the grieving duo Beckstein-Huber had taken office.
The Social Christians for several decades were developing Bavaria - and now it's precisely these modern citizens who turn their backs on it.
The change of an era is at the same time a problem of the entire Christian democracy, as well as by Angela Merkel.
The failure wasn't entirely surprising for the CSU, but on Sunday a process that has already been planned for almost ten years was completed: the undisputed hegemony of a state party disappeared.
Ten years ago in 1998, the CSU gained 47.7 per cent, for the first time below 50 per cent since the fifties.
This erupted three years ago, in the 2005 Bundestag elections, i.e. even during Stoiber's well-mentioned era.
The reason for this is the consequent modernization of the CSU in the devouring province of its parents .
Sociologists have been predicting this for 40 years, but at the pace they were very wrong. However, the secularization, the results of the education and settlement of the Northern Germans in the developed regions of Bavaria had its negative consequences on the fidelity of CSU electors.
Modernization also increases the possibilities and this also has political consequences.
The right-wing group is untouched, only within the group the most conscious, most critical and most stubborn electors became.
Unsurprisingly in the last few weeks the conventional groups of the citizens moved the most away from the CSU; the self-employed, the small industrialists, those who have a baccalaureate and graduates.
That's how support for the CSU was recreated to less than 40 percent.
Die Presse (diepresse.com)
The Conservative newspaper of Vienna: "Who with whom? In its article entitled "The presidents of the parties are very reserved on coalition issues" - deals with the confusing situation of domestic politics after the Austrian elections.
The company wants to negotiate with the VP.
The VP forbids the SPE from meddling in its internal affairs.
It is open in all directions to negotiate, the Greens instead do not yet wish to consult on the coalition.
Already the elections were held, now everything revolves around the coalition; in the debate held on public television, the party leaders barely showed some of their decks.
Werner Faymann, the president of the SP, confirmed that he wants to negotiate with the VP.
It wants to show that it is possible to collaborate between the two parties - it already exists in some provinces and social associations.
Fayman left pending the question that if you can imagine a coalition with Wilhelm Molterer, the current president of the VP, in the post of vice-chancellor.
He stated that in any case they disappropriated the Schsssel-Molterer era.
It is not possible to pursue this political trend.
Molterer relapsed cautiously to the red offers.
He said co-operation will surely not be successful if Faymann tries to meddle in the internal affairs of VP.
It is still open in the party if they will elect the opposition or join the coalition, these issues must certainly be debated, within VP itself.
The President of the BZ, J.rg Haider was open in all directions.
"In order to prevent the red-black coalition, we are prepared to assume the responsibility of the government," Haider said.
Járóka: The vicious cycle of poverty and segregation must be shattered
The European Summit for Equal Opportunities has been launched today in Paris, its host is France, which this year is also the current president of the European Union.
Lívia Járóka, Member of the European People &apos; s Party for Fidesz, in her message addressed to the plenary, as one of the main speakers of this professional meeting, drew attention to the segregation in education, because it is one of the most serious violations plaguing Gypsy children and it is also the greatest obstacle in the face of the propensity of their interests.
This high-level conference was inaugurated - on behalf of the French Presidency - by Xavier Bertrand, French Minister of Employment and Social Affairs and Vladimir Spidla, European commissioner responsible for jobs, social affairs and equal opportunities.
The meeting was attended by representatives of governments, the European social partners and representatives of the most important European social rights protection organisations.
Járóka - according to the press bureau of the Fidesz Group of Members in the European Parliament - warned that in addition to discrimination in employment, Gypsies are left out of the labour market in the first place because of their low level of schooling.
"Most young gypsies do not have completed secondary education and due to the poor quality of basic education, only an insignificant part can obtain a university diploma."
Járóka also stressed that education, as a fundamental human right, has a decisive role to have a decisive role in the protection of other human rights as well.
Investment in the education of Roma children at a young age can strengthen the economy of the Member States of the Member States of the European Union.
According to the MEP, it is necessary to raise a wide awareness of these issues among both Gypsies and non-gypsies.
