CSU: grace for a transitional period
At the end of October there will be a decision on the fate of the failed Huber-Beckstein duo
In spite of the serious electoral defeat, heads have not yet been severed at the heights of the Bavarian CSU, for the time being the debate on personal issues has been postponed.
Erwin Huber, president of the party and Gonther Beckstein, provincial prime minister, still remain in their posts, but it is not known how long the grace will last.
The fate of the duo, which suffered a catastrophic 17 per cent election defeat, will be decided at the party's extraordinary congress to be held on the last weekend in October.
The members of the much-criticized duo glimpsed: they don't clinch their posts so much.
With this they intended to reassure the angry members of their parliamentary group and of their grassroots organization, who one day after the "Bavarian tsunami" demanded personal consequences due to the loss of an absolute majority.
Five years ago - albeit under Edmund Stoiber who was at the height of his career - the Social Christian Union reached 60.7 per per cent, instead now, a year after the beating streak against Stoiber, they achieved just 43.4 per cent.
The CSU for 46 years had "monopoly" in Bavaria that gives 18 per cent of Germany's GDP.
Although in the province of the South, of key importance to Hungary's economy, the Conservatives continue to dominate, in the future they will undoubtedly have to coalition with someone.
As a first sign of the party's reshuffle, Vice-President Horst Seehofer, a federal agriculture minister, one of Huber's possible successors, also has a seat in the small intelligence group that begins negotiations with the liberal FDP and right-wing Free Electors over a possible government coalition in Munich.
The bizarre thing about fate is that on the Free Elecators' list includes Gabriele Pauli, whose performance against Stoiber was tapped for the Huber-Beckstein duo and then cruelly erased ambitious local politics from the stage.
The CSU's top leaders are now reversing all their energies to stabilizing Beckstein.
In the transition he will also receive an active role Stoiber, who so far has been pushing the strings in the background, the former head of provincial government and former CSU president described September 28 as the most bitter day of his political career.
Huber on Monday made some critical remarks to the CDU, in his view would have deserved far more support from the sister party.
Other CSU politicians interpreted the defeat as a warning for the CDU and Angela Merkel herself too.
After the Bundestag's September 2009 elections, Merkel will not be able to become chancellor if they are not able to fully rebranch the CSU that played a momentous role in the successes of the 2005 national elections.
According to Merkel who could barely hide her displeasure from the failure, the CSU's losses will not have a negative impact on the Great Berlin coalition.
Which of course is relative: it won't be any easier to treat the wounded CSU and the social democrat's crisis continues.
The 18.6 per cent SPD achieved its worst result since 1946 in Bavaria.
Lukashenko Opposition: 110-0
Behind Sunday's elections, there will be no opponent in the Belarusian parliament.
According to the Russian observers, this is explained by the well-being and stability guaranteed in Alexandr Lukashenko, on the other hand, according to the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE), this is rather explained by violations of the law committed during the vote count.
The OSCE, which dispatched 450 observers from 43 countries, did not recognize the electoral outcome.
Although the Legislature of the country of 10 million inhabitants, made up of one per cent and 10 deputies, more than a decade ago it had no opposition, before yesterday's elections the united opposition under the name of the United Democratic Forces (ODSZ) expected Lukashenko, who for the fourth time renewed his mandate after the 2006 elections of doubtful legitimacy, as a result of the amendment of the Constitution this time "allow" the entry of some opposition deputies into parliament.
Thus the ODSZ suppressed the boycott it originally wanted to do.
At the heart of the opposition &apos; s expectations was that Belarus, which is becoming increasingly economically reliant on Russia - which took credit for moderating the increasingly expensive import of natural gas from Russia and which contributed to the sale to Russia of the Beltransgaz, owner of the pipeline that passes through the country - wanted to improve its relations with the European Union, which does not give Lukashenko and the other high-level politicians permission for having a political regime considered anti-democratic.
It was a sign of detente when the OSCE, in assessing the election campaign, placed more emphasis on the positive phenomena and not on mistakes - such as the ban on taxing one-and-a-third of opposition candidates.
However, Monday's OSCE primary report criticized even the campaign itself - in which opposition candidates could not appear in the press - and especially that the Opposition could not be present at the vote count.
With all this in the opinion of the opposition there is an improvement in the political climate: the political prisoners were released and Minsk is ready for a small confrontation with Moscow, denying the recognition of the separation of South Ossetia and Abhasia from Georgia, although Russia - with which Belarus theoretically constitutes a federal state - has already considered the two regions as independent.
"We have taken two steps forward and one backwards, but the most important thing is that the dialogue between Belarus and the European Union has been initiated," Alexandr Milinkevich told our newspaper.
According to the opposition candidate of the 2006 presidential elections it is desirable to maintain this situation, even though "the regime is afraid to allow even an opponent to enter that weightless parliament."
The opposition doesn't plan to organize protest demonstrations when they see the results, rather it tries to convey to the voters its message about the need for economic reforms.
"This clearly causes us a lot of problems because we have to impress the opposition press in Russia, the local authorities face difficulties in their distribution, but even so I see that more and more people support change among the adults as well," said Milinkevich, alluding to Lukashenko's regime, who believes that its main result is the creation of social security, does not consider it appropriate even retirees, those who are officially the most subsidised by the regime.
"Because of the country's indebtedness and the increase of the trade deficit, it's only a matter of time for the decomposition of social security that "resembles cemetery silence," Milinkevich said.
Russian observers, opposed to the opposition politician, described the elections as democratic.
Serguei Lebedev, head of the mission of the Commonwealth of Independent States told the Russian news agency Ria Novosti: "People don't believe in words but in deeds," so the lack of the opposition is none other than the testimony of the Maturity of Belarusian voters.
