We must also re-examine the existing control systems in the light of this scientific opinion, so that they are very effective and take the step with the new technology."
Bruxel had asked the Independent Scientific Committee to examine the problem, looking at the great use of portable musical devices and an increase in the number of youngsters exposed to the noise it causes.
"Scientists - it ends the executive Ue and confirm that it is a matter of concern and the European Commission will now examine together with the Member States and interested parties that measures could eventually be taken in order to better protect the children and the teens from exposure to noise coming from portable musical devices and related apparatuses."
At present, there is already a European safety standard that limits the noise level of portable musical devices to 100 dB, but concern increases such as auditory damage that can result from excessive exposure to such noise sources.
It is possible to greatly limit these damages by resorting to solutions such as reducing levels and noise exposure time.
In its opinion, the Ue Scientific Committee notes that users of portable musical devices are listening at a high volume (more than 89 decibels)music for only 5 hours a week exceed the current limit for maximum noise consented at work.
Those listening to music in such conditions for longer periods risk a permanent loss of auditory ability after a period of five years.
It means that it affects 5-10% of the users of such devices and the number can be quantified between 2.5 and 10 million people in the U.
