Endangered elites
The phenomenon had already been manifested a few days ago by the vote of the representatives against the $700-billion salvation plan launched by the White House: a significant part of the Americans was, more interested in punishing Wall Street rather than saving the economy.
The New York Times confirms yesterday informing us that the ongoing crisis is disarming the Republican party, always more divided between its old soul of the East Coast, moderate, favorable to the federal institutions and the business community, and its new soul, on the other hand, Conservative, harassing "those of Washington" and the world of finance, strong especially in the Central and Southern states.
The soul, in fact, has become arrogant with the vow of those who were speaking at the beginning.
This barely quoted is just an example of the mutations, of large mutations, which the current economic earthquake announces: not only in the United States but in all the West and not only here alone.
Either in the United States as in Europe the crisis seems to function as an acccelerator of incubation phenomena that with the new climate solidify and come to light.
The first of these phenomena is the re-addiction, the extraordinary reactivation, of the Double State-sovereignty category in relation to a type of renationalization of the economy.
The crisis, in fact, is a crisis of banking institutions - it would finance which, yeah, have strong financial relations with abroad, but above all the balance sheets of families living in a single country and in a single state are much more compromised.
Whoever immediately creates a political problem for those who govern it: that is to say how to respond to the difficulty and protests of those people and those families who, on the other hand, are electors.
The crisis appears economically global but it is politically and exclusively national.
Politician internationalism seems substantially out-of-the-game or they don't have much to say: the crucial test is The European Union that is divided as always between the different interests and the different state strategies fails to decide any policy in common.
It belongs to the government of each state that everyone expects an intervention, rescue and reactivation plans, of new rules and above all the subsidy of funds: from the moment it comes to the cut, it is only the state that possesses the economic resources and the mass of resources to finance you to remedy things.
And they also have the necessary means and the legitimacy to be used: two incalculable value resources, values in certain circumstances in which no market probably and no organization will ever be able to dispose to the same extent.
This new emphasis that the crisis places on the nation-state is in perfect tune, with the utmost importance as long as the new development of the economy tends to attribute a very collapsing factor to that element: territoriality.
Paradoxically, while we were convinced that we had entered the network of ever more sophisticated, immaterial technology, while we were convinced that global finance was already destined to dominate the world, we realized that our future will instead have to do the belts more and more with unimmaterial things such as water, crops and oil.
Everything that, you see, can certainly be transported from one place to the next of the earth, but they are equally unclocked in a circumscribed space, a territory.
So whoever finds himself as a sovereign certainly possesses many more letters about who he isn't, and to whom he has the misfortune of living in a stall without crops, without oil and without water.
I come to the second novelty that but foreshadows a fracturing.
Those who say Territory, State, government, inevitably says Politics and Leadership.
In this sense, the economic crisis seems to produce two converging phenomena.
On the one hand the objective awareness of the need for a leadership, the request of someone who knows how to take the situation in hand.
On the other hand a wave of disreputation for the existing leadership, of an economically remedied species inadequate with many dark areas.
And together something deeper and in a sense disturbing: a disgrace, an intolerance, an inadequate image, a sense of remoteness, which tends to involve the entire ruling class of Western countries in a growing number.
Always more seems to advance, in various places of the population, the conviction that before their actions are the same as those of the social elites so far booming, the same way of feeling and of being, their culture in the sympathetic exception of the end, having done one's own time and being stranger to the opinions of the majority.
The demand for leadership, in a nutshell-and-devely, subterranean movement feeds a strengthened movement of the ruling classes and traditional political structures, manifesting themselves in an "revolt-turn" or in the revolt of collective moods of recent times, from the conservative departure in Austria-Baviera to the collapse of social-democratic consensus in Great Britain, the rebellion of American congress men against Bush (while also with the victory of the right in Italy that has something to do with it).
There is an impression that the traditional elites, their parties, their programs, but their rites, their newspapers, their accredited intellectuals, their format would say any, will always make it more difficult to comprehens themselves and then to represent, what from today that is taking shape are the deep strata of Western society and that the economic crisis renews, magnifises and sweeps potentially excessive.
Faced with all this, talking about a "mass rebellion" today is frankly exaggerated.
But keeping your eyes open certainly isn't.
