Expensive rental students, up to 900 euros for a bedroom
Rental market for university students doesn't know about the crisis.
According to Sunia's latest survey, the tenants' syndicate, renting a single bedroom I was able to cost a student 900 euros in Milan, $700 in Furniture, $650 to Napoles, 600 to Rome.
"the phenomenon has already assumed very serious characteristics to the point of excluding the right to study a large part of young people because of the very high costs that the family would have to bear," explains Laura Mariani's head of the studio office, Laura Mariani - in these cities the strong demand has totally deformed the market to such an extent to triggering a process of widespread increase even for the residents."
Apart from the fact that in most cases rental offers contain a series of violations: free-type contracts, unrepaired, with no fee limit, sub-requisition linked.
Extraordinary interventions are needed for Sunia that, through the review of tax taxation, prices can be regulated.
Looking in detail at the survey, we see that in Milan, for a bed in the Brianza area, they serve approximately 450 euros; for a single room from a minimum of 650 ( Bande Nere area) at an average value of 800 (Lambiate Zone, Udine and Fiera) at a maximum of 900 (in Victoria area).
In Florence a bed costs an average of 350/400 euros, a bedroom approximately 700 euros.
In Bologna, where the students are concentrated in the areas close to the University, they serve 250/280 euros for a bed in a double bed and from 370 to 500 for an individual.
In Rome in the central areas close to the University(San Lorenzo, Plaza Bologna) you are asked for approximately 600 euros for a single room and 450 for a bed in a double room.
Only slightly lower values (550 euros for a single room) in areas close to other Universities(Ostiense and Cinecitt).
It becomes economy only if the peripheral area is changed: 300 euros for one bed and 450 for a single bed in Prenestina, Centocelle and surrounding areas.
In Naples for a bed you are served 300/450 euros, for a bedroom it is spent from 400 to 600 euros, with the highest prices in the polyclinic, Vomero and Colli Aminei areas.
In Bari by a bed you are served 250/350 euros, at least 350 per individual.
In the smaller cities the prices are lower in absolute finishes, but it carries a greater weight in the city's economy.
In cities such as in Perugia, where the phenomenon of student and non-municipal rentals is more frequent, there is a subsequent anomaly: an increase of 25/30% of the fee for foreign students compared to Italian students.
Good-bye to the old Paradise cinema, the city cancels the historic halls
Historic movie theaters close favoring the Multiplex.
"It's a city with no movie theaters in a blind city, it's like an eye-free face".
The image offered by director Giuseppe Tornatore photographs effectively what's going on in the big Italian urban centers and also in the provincial cities.
The process of marginalization of the cinemas, which with the birth of the great meticons on the periphery and the consequent disappearance of monkey rooms in the central areas of the city, is an inevitable and indisputable event with all the characteristics of a true cultural crime.
To disappear are almost always historical places, full of memorabilia, "wans as if a friend died, an acquaint of an acquaintance with whom you shared a piece of place and so much excitement," observes the director of the new film Paradise, the film that twenty years ago won the Oscar cousin and the grand jury prize to Cannes, who counted the poetry of a provincial cinema and the nostalgia for the way of living the cinema he lost maybe forever.
The figures given by the Anec, the main association of the merchants, speak clearly: in the last five years they have closed 316 traditional theatrical cinemas, and to pay for the majority are above all the cinemas of the main cities: 10 fewer in Florence, 9 less in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 fewer in Turin and 5 in Palermo.
Among the regions most suffered by the closures are as the first, Emilia-Romagna with 82 fewer cinemas, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
It is a demonstration that the person responsible for his disappearance is the adventure of the multiplex, these three Italian regions have recorded the largest number of new Methones cinemas: 195 more screens in Lombardy in the last five years, 81 either in Tuscany than in Emilia-Romagna.
"The Italian exercise has unerrationally" observes Paolo Protti, president of the Anec," it would be useful if new structures were to be built but it would be necessary to ensure the permanence of those already existing, because when a city room closes, not all its attendees are willing to transfer to the meticons.
Not to mention the lack of funds in favour of traders: the state's previous debt is approximately 10 million euros.
It's embarrassing from an ethical and moral point of view that the government doesn't respect the laws that exist.
Sustaining the sector would serve between EUR 16 and EUR 18 million a year.
Interesting in the case of Bologna, the first Italian city that worried about the closures, it decided to be repaired with the signature of a protocol between the municipality and Anec, the first of its kind in Italy to promote and protect the monkey cinema room is in the historic center that on the periphery.
Among the established rules there are tax facilitation mediations in favour of managers and advantages for the public in mobility completions: spectators will be able to enter the limited traffic zone and will have special parking fees.
Giuseppe Tornatore observes" and I hope that other cities will follow the example of Bologna by setting other measures.
It's true, that in order to contrast the extra power of the meticons, the traditional rooms have to improve the quality of the sound and the image because the audience today is more demanding.
The defense of traditional cinema is a theme that for me was always important: the closing of Bagheria's cinema, the place of my childhood and adolescence, was the spark that persuaded me to make the "New Paradise Cinema," which I had already thought for 10 years.
I've lived in Rome for 25 years now, and when I walk past the Rialto or the Quirinale de vía Nazionale, where I've experienced so many shared emotions, so I don't see the posters blackened by the dust, I turn myself to the other side.
