When the energy is born from the waves, the big ones come down to the stage
It is an exterminated site that occupies more than 70% of the planet's surface and is discovering it now, the world's big energy companies.
Waves, streams, tides are the new renewable sources you look at the greats like Chevron.
The possibility of collecting energy from sea movements has been studied for a hundred years and in the 1970s together with the first oil crisis, investigations intensified.
When the price of low oil, in 1980, the idea of taking advantage of marine energy was diverted, but now that cases of the environment and the new increase in oil give impetus to the sector.
London's World Energy Council (WEC) has estimated 15 per per cent of the world's electrical need could be covered by implants exploiting the movement of sea waves.
This amount would be twice that currently produced by the nuclear power plants.
And the currents and tides would also be there.
Giants to the attack.
The first company to bet on marine energy has been Enersis, which in 2005 has sealed an agreement to build an implant in northern Portugal.
When the intention was announced it seemed like a revolutionary step.
Twisted two years away from betting on marine energy it is always and among these companies that have always voted for the traditional energy.
"Chevron is studying technology on ocean energy," said the spokesman for a company associated with the U.S. oil colossus to the economic newspaper Wall Street Journal and is assessing the possibility of cooperating with us."
The co-operation in question is to obtain a permit from the U.S. energy commission to carry out a project to exploit marine energy in Alaska.
It's a fairly important sign, if you consider that in Alaska the U.S. continues to extract oil and President Bush has recently activated a plan for new drunkenness.
In addition to Chevron is Verdant Power Inc. of New York and New Jersey's Ocean Power to follow the path of energy that comes from the sea, the total invested so far, even if they are not detailed, indicate an increasing interest indicates an increasing interest.
Technology to develop.
Analysts talk about a technology still at the beginning, because there is no doubt that it has a great environmental impact.
At the same time the impacts such as Portuguese or the Scouts are of a small dimension and don't seem to create big problems for the ecosystem.
To produce long-scale energy, a technology with a greater environmental impact must be used.
And the options are several: Ocean Power, for example, uses a system of boas joined between them.
Strength of the waves, which move the boas up and down, becomes hydraulic pressure with pisstones and relinels inside them.
The pressure spins a turbine that in turn fuels a generator.
Electricity is shipped to shore through submarine cables.
At the moment there are four implants of this type in development, waiting only for federal approval, in addition to the energy, it could serve for a military facility.
Verdant Power, on the other hand, already produces energy for a shopping mall and a car park, using underwater turbines on New York's East River.
The movement of the water when there's tides, spins the pallets of turbines, creating a rotating movement that feeds the generator.
Verdant has said that he has a long-running cast of customers waiting for the necessary authorisations to use the energy.
Behind the Waves, the tides.
A particular interest, they say at the Institute for the development of electrical energy, at the moment merits the potential of tides.
Although it is an intermittent phenomenon, tides are more preventable than the wind, the sun or the waves and therefore more reliable as a source of energy.
It's above all about tidal energy that states such as New York, Maine and Alaska, along with others approaching the shores, have invested an average of $7.5 million in 2008 and an investment of $35 million is planned for 2009.
